1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,964
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,964 --> 00:00:03,916
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,916 --> 00:00:06,356
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,466 --> 00:00:18,000
SARA BEERY: OK, so welcome to lecture two of deep learning.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,240
Today, we're going to talk about how you actually

6
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,760
train a neural network.

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:25,780
And I imagine that some of this might be review for some of you

8
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:27,880
but, hopefully, the perspective or the way

9
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:31,000
we talk about the process of training the neural network

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,400
might be a slightly reformulated from something

11
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:34,440
you've seen before.

12
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,440
I definitely think that this slide is a nice way

13
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:39,140
to characterize the process.

14
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,960
So today, we're going to talk about how you actually

15
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,065
train a neural network.

16
00:00:44,065 --> 00:00:45,440
First, we're going to start doing

17
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,480
a bit of a review of gradient descent SGD, high-level,

18
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:51,690
some optimization ideas.

19
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,440
Then, we're going to introduce the concept

20
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,520
of a computational graph.

21
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:58,200
We'll talk about backpropagation through chains

22
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,480
and through multi-layer perceptrons.

23
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:04,000
We'll talk about backpropagation through DAGs.

24
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,880
And then we will get into the concept of-- sorry--

25
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:10,080
differential programming.

26
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:14,783
OK, so just, again, as a bit of a refresher, when

27
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:16,200
we talk about neural network, it's

28
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:21,080
usually something that follows the rough structure of this,

29
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,080
where you will have some input data x.

30
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:29,040
And during training time, you'll have some ground truth

31
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,140
for that input data.

32
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:32,760
This is the thing that you want the model to learn to predict.

33
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:35,380
So in this particular instance, that is a clownfish.

34
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:36,880
You're asking this model to predict,

35
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,960
maybe, a visual category, and that's y.

36
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:41,960
And then we have some model.

37
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:45,920
This is a chain of different blocks or layers

38
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,600
of a neural network.

39
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:51,480
Each of those layers will have parameters associated with them.

40
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,360
And then once you send this input

41
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:54,880
through all of those layers, you'll

42
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,670
calculate a loss based on the difference between what

43
00:01:57,670 --> 00:02:00,270
the model predicts and that ground truth.

44
00:02:00,270 --> 00:02:02,235
And all of those parameters that are

45
00:02:02,235 --> 00:02:04,110
going through the network, those are actually

46
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:05,590
what's getting learned.

47
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:07,850
And what when you say getting learned,

48
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:09,789
essentially, we just mean we wiggle the values

49
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:12,750
around in numerical space until we get something

50
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:16,950
that is at least reasonably close to optimal,

51
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:22,870
based on a set of data that we actually have access to.

52
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:23,390
Cool.

53
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:25,870
So essentially what you're trying to find

54
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:28,550
is some optimal set of theta that

55
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:34,350
minimizes that loss function over all of your available data

56
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:35,870
set.

57
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:38,950
Now, when we talk about gradient descent,

58
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:45,510
here, finding that optimal set of parameters, what we often

59
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:47,830
will talk about is that that's going

60
00:02:47,830 --> 00:02:54,070
to be the set of parameters that is optimal for some cost.

61
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:56,550
In this particular sense, that cost function

62
00:02:56,550 --> 00:02:59,430
is the sum of the loss over all the data points in our training

63
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:01,717
data set.

64
00:03:01,717 --> 00:03:04,050
And then how do we actually fiddle those weights around?

65
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:06,070
What's the mechanism?

66
00:03:06,070 --> 00:03:08,950
That's really just using optimization

67
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:11,870
to try to find those optimal parameters for that cost

68
00:03:11,870 --> 00:03:13,230
function.

69
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:16,390
So what's the knowledge that we have about J?

70
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:21,110
Well, first, we know that we can actually evaluate J of theta

71
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:22,233
for any given theta.

72
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:24,650
So this is something that we can actually evaluate, right?

73
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:27,510
You can calculate the loss over a training data set or a test

74
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:29,310
data set.

75
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:34,830
We also know that we can evaluate the gradient of theta.

76
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:37,750
And this is first order optimization.

77
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:40,430
You might take some approximation of the gradient

78
00:03:40,430 --> 00:03:42,450
based on a specific point.

79
00:03:42,450 --> 00:03:44,190
And you can evaluate that as well.

80
00:03:44,190 --> 00:03:46,070
And, additionally, we can also do something

81
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:49,110
like evaluating second order optimization, where you could

82
00:03:49,110 --> 00:03:50,950
actually calculate the Hessian.

83
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:52,390
In practice, this isn't something

84
00:03:52,390 --> 00:03:54,580
that we often actually do.

85
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:58,100
Usually, we really just focus on that first and then

86
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:00,140
that first order optimization, where we're just

87
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:02,300
looking at that linear approximation

88
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:05,700
to the gradient at a given point.

89
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:07,500
And then what is gradient descent?

90
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:10,340
So if you're trying to find this theta star--

91
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:12,725
that's this optimal value--

92
00:04:12,725 --> 00:04:14,100
what you're going to do is you're

93
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:16,660
going to start somewhere on what we call the loss

94
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:20,260
landscape or the surface that represents that cost function.

95
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,580
And then you're going to move in the direction

96
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:25,860
of the gradient, the maximal gradient,

97
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:28,140
and takes steps over your data.

98
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,380
And so for each of these, you'll start

99
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:33,540
moving in that downward direction on that loss landscape

100
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:35,452
and eventually find a minimum.

101
00:04:35,452 --> 00:04:37,660
Now of course, you might note, if this loss landscape

102
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,580
is non-convex, if it's quite complex

103
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:41,500
and maybe has multiple minima, this

104
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,960
may not be guaranteed to be the global minimum.

105
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:46,020
It might just be a local minimum.

106
00:04:46,020 --> 00:04:47,860
And how bad that local minimum is

107
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:52,420
relative to the global minimum, often, we don't know.

108
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:55,500
So if you assume you're taking a single iteration

109
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:59,980
of gradient descent and trying to find better parameters,

110
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:04,620
what we'll do is we will start with our k-th step

111
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:06,820
of those parameters theta, and then

112
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:10,940
we will define some learning rate, in this case eta,

113
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:12,620
and that's going to say how far we're

114
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:15,140
going to step in the direction of that gradient.

115
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:17,700
And then you calculate, essentially,

116
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:23,900
what is the direction of change that will optimize that loss

117
00:05:23,900 --> 00:05:25,460
or that cost function.

118
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:29,020
So now what you do is you take the parameters of your model,

119
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:32,100
and you move them in the direction

120
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,620
of the maximal slope of your gradient

121
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:38,920
with some of learning rate step value.

122
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:42,260
And that gives you the update to your parameters.

123
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:46,700
Now, this might be something that you could reasonably

124
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:50,330
calculate over all of your existing training data.

125
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:54,690
You'd like to, ideally, minimize the overall loss function,

126
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:57,010
which would be some sum of the individual losses

127
00:05:57,010 --> 00:06:00,210
over every single example in your training data.

128
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:03,770
But realistically, this is often not possible to calculate,

129
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:07,290
mostly just due to the time, the computational complexity,

130
00:06:07,290 --> 00:06:10,610
especially in this day and age when data sets are

131
00:06:10,610 --> 00:06:12,530
getting larger and larger.

132
00:06:12,530 --> 00:06:15,470
So when I started in machine learning,

133
00:06:15,470 --> 00:06:18,170
we were lucky if we had ImageNet scale data

134
00:06:18,170 --> 00:06:19,890
sets of a million images.

135
00:06:19,890 --> 00:06:23,010
Now we're dealing with image data sets in the billions

136
00:06:23,010 --> 00:06:26,490
and text data sets even larger than that.

137
00:06:26,490 --> 00:06:29,890
So instead, in stochastic gradient descent,

138
00:06:29,890 --> 00:06:32,490
you assume that taking the gradient

139
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:35,250
over some subset of your data is a reasonable approximation

140
00:06:35,250 --> 00:06:38,070
to taking the gradient over the entire data set.

141
00:06:38,070 --> 00:06:39,570
So here what you do is, instead, you

142
00:06:39,570 --> 00:06:43,030
compute a gradient on a subset of the data at any given point.

143
00:06:43,030 --> 00:06:45,050
And we call this a batch.

144
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:47,570
So if your batch size is 1, what that would mean

145
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:50,130
is you would actually update your parameters

146
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:53,450
after seeing every single example in the data set.

147
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:55,650
You can imagine that also might be

148
00:06:55,650 --> 00:06:57,850
suboptimal in terms of the computational complexity

149
00:06:57,850 --> 00:06:59,690
of doing those updates.

150
00:06:59,690 --> 00:07:02,830
If your batch size is N, where N is the full set of data,

151
00:07:02,830 --> 00:07:05,170
then this is just standard gradient descent.

152
00:07:05,170 --> 00:07:06,670
But of course, it might be possible.

153
00:07:06,670 --> 00:07:09,210
This is an easy to fit into the memory of your GPU,

154
00:07:09,210 --> 00:07:13,610
or might just be really slow, computationally intractable.

155
00:07:13,610 --> 00:07:16,450
So also, if your gradient direction

156
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:20,930
is kind of noisy relative to averaging over

157
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:26,410
all of the examples, which is standard gradient descent,

158
00:07:26,410 --> 00:07:27,890
this is pretty normal.

159
00:07:27,890 --> 00:07:31,770
So taking a batch, you're not necessarily guaranteed

160
00:07:31,770 --> 00:07:34,290
that gradient over the batch is going to match

161
00:07:34,290 --> 00:07:35,850
the gradient over everything.

162
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:38,250
And actually in a lot of the data that I work with,

163
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:41,130
where we have things like significant imbalance

164
00:07:41,130 --> 00:07:45,410
over the types of categories we're interested in predicting,

165
00:07:45,410 --> 00:07:47,920
this sampling can have a pretty strong effect in terms

166
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:49,560
of how stable that gradient is.

167
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:52,560
Because potentially you have some categories

168
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:55,440
that are not seen for multiple batches at a time,

169
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:57,100
and then you see them.

170
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:00,000
And at that point, it actually massively shifts your gradient.

171
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,560
So this instability is actually often related

172
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:08,880
to how uniformly distributed your data set is.

173
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:12,640
But there are advantages of doing that stochastic gradient

174
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:13,400
descent.

175
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:15,240
First, it's faster.

176
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:17,920
It's been shown to approximate the total gradient,

177
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:20,120
even with small samples.

178
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:23,860
And that noise, it's actually an implicit regularizer.

179
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:27,360
So one thing that can be a benefit of stochastic gradient

180
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:31,240
descent is it can actually bounce you out of local minima

181
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:35,320
and help you reasonably find closer to what

182
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:37,559
might be a global minimum.

183
00:08:37,559 --> 00:08:39,440
There are disadvantages.

184
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:41,659
Like we said, there can be high variance.

185
00:08:41,659 --> 00:08:43,140
There can be unstable updates.

186
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:45,960
And I've found experimentally that these updates are

187
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:49,320
more unstable the more unbalanced

188
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:51,800
your training data set is over the set of categories

189
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:54,480
that you're interested to predict on.

190
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:55,020
Cool.

191
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:57,160
Any questions about SGD?

192
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:59,960
How many of you have seen SGD before?

193
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,040
OK, that's what I thought.

194
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:05,960
So this is, hopefully, a bit of a refresher for most of you.

195
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:10,640
So we also want to introduce the concept of momentum.

196
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:15,080
And this is something that we might implement within our loss

197
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:18,040
or within our gradient descent.

198
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:20,600
So, essentially, the idea of momentum

199
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:23,260
is the physical idea of momentum.

200
00:09:23,260 --> 00:09:25,780
If you put something heavy on a slope,

201
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:28,720
it will gain speed as it rolls down the slope.

202
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,640
And what we do when we think about

203
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:33,120
momentum in gradient descent is we,

204
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:35,360
essentially, are biasing our gradient steps

205
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:38,100
to continue in the direction of the previous update.

206
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:41,600
You're giving it that momentum based on its past direction

207
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:42,417
of movement.

208
00:09:42,417 --> 00:09:44,750
And so, here, what that looks like is you're essentially

209
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:47,670
just adding in, again, this parameterized

210
00:09:47,670 --> 00:09:51,310
by alpha momentum term that is capturing the direction that you

211
00:09:51,310 --> 00:09:52,990
moved in the past.

212
00:09:52,990 --> 00:09:56,313
And this can actually help or hurt.

213
00:09:56,313 --> 00:09:58,230
And the strength of that momentum, that alpha,

214
00:09:58,230 --> 00:09:59,910
is a hyperparameter.

215
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:06,670
A popular example of this type of momentum in gradient descent

216
00:10:06,670 --> 00:10:10,110
is Adam, if any of you have heard of the Adam optimizer.

217
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:12,910
So this is something that is pretty commonly used.

218
00:10:12,910 --> 00:10:15,470
But how this might actually help or hurt--

219
00:10:15,470 --> 00:10:18,550
so here, this is just looking at optimizing for something

220
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:20,390
that is quite sharp.

221
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:22,870
And you can see that if the momentum is 0,

222
00:10:22,870 --> 00:10:27,150
you'll kind of roll steadily towards that maximum.

223
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:29,710
If you actually have a momentum of 0.5,

224
00:10:29,710 --> 00:10:33,910
you're getting to that minimum much quicker,

225
00:10:33,910 --> 00:10:37,390
maybe about half the time the number of training steps.

226
00:10:37,390 --> 00:10:41,450
But if your momentum is too high, you start bouncing past,

227
00:10:41,450 --> 00:10:43,950
and then you have this almost oscillation and wiggling

228
00:10:43,950 --> 00:10:46,370
that happens as you're trying to find that minimum.

229
00:10:46,370 --> 00:10:50,110
And it ends up taking longer to optimize your function.

230
00:10:50,110 --> 00:10:54,070
And there's a really nice blog post on momentum

231
00:10:54,070 --> 00:10:58,087
that has some really fun interactive capacity.

232
00:10:58,087 --> 00:11:00,170
If you guys are interested, the link is down here.

233
00:11:00,170 --> 00:11:01,610
It's from back in 2017.

234
00:11:01,610 --> 00:11:05,430
But I found this really helpful for building intuition.

235
00:11:05,430 --> 00:11:06,150
Cool.

236
00:11:06,150 --> 00:11:13,390
So talking about loss landscapes, which of these

237
00:11:13,390 --> 00:11:16,830
are differentiable?

238
00:11:16,830 --> 00:11:18,350
So raise of hands.

239
00:11:18,350 --> 00:11:21,390
Give me one that's differentiable.

240
00:11:21,390 --> 00:11:23,290
Yes?

241
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:24,550
AUDIENCE: The first one.

242
00:11:24,550 --> 00:11:25,633
SARA BEERY: The first one.

243
00:11:25,633 --> 00:11:27,005
Yeah, the top--

244
00:11:27,005 --> 00:11:27,630
AUDIENCE: Left.

245
00:11:27,630 --> 00:11:28,670
SARA BEERY: --left, yes.

246
00:11:28,670 --> 00:11:29,330
Thank you.

247
00:11:29,330 --> 00:11:32,110
Right and left-- still hard for me.

248
00:11:32,110 --> 00:11:33,050
Yeah.

249
00:11:33,050 --> 00:11:34,630
AUDIENCE: The third one?

250
00:11:34,630 --> 00:11:35,670
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

251
00:11:35,670 --> 00:11:37,330
The third one, exactly it's flat,

252
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:40,480
but it does have a derivative.

253
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:41,360
So, yeah, exactly.

254
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,140
These two are differentiable.

255
00:11:44,140 --> 00:11:49,832
So now, which of these have defined gradients in PyTorch.

256
00:11:49,832 --> 00:11:51,273
AUDIENCE: All of them.

257
00:11:51,273 --> 00:11:52,440
SARA BEERY: Raise your hand.

258
00:11:55,340 --> 00:11:56,020
Yes?

259
00:11:56,020 --> 00:11:57,180
AUDIENCE: Probably all of them, right?

260
00:11:57,180 --> 00:11:57,847
SARA BEERY: Yes.

261
00:11:57,847 --> 00:12:00,540
All of them have defined gradients in PyTorch.

262
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:02,620
That's because PyTorch has autograd.

263
00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:05,660
It has this nice mechanism that helps us calculate

264
00:12:05,660 --> 00:12:07,700
gradients for any function.

265
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:10,520
Which of them would be hard to optimize over?

266
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:17,220
Yeah?

267
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:18,580
AUDIENCE: Second one.

268
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:19,660
SARA BEERY: Why?

269
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:21,655
AUDIENCE: It's got multiple local minima.

270
00:12:21,655 --> 00:12:23,280
SARA BEERY: Yep, multiple local minima.

271
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:26,140
So you might end up, depending on where you start,

272
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:27,640
not in the global minimum.

273
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:31,620
Yeah?

274
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:33,620
AUDIENCE: The third and the fourth, since they're both flat.

275
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:36,203
SARA BEERY: The third and the fourth, since they're both flat.

276
00:12:36,203 --> 00:12:39,200
Yeah, so the third one is just flat everywhere.

277
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:41,800
Essentially, there's no signal in terms of the gradient.

278
00:12:41,800 --> 00:12:43,500
The fourth one, it's flat everywhere

279
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:46,960
except for discontinuity, but anywhere on the actual graph.

280
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:49,260
Again, you have no signal.

281
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:50,500
Yeah?

282
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:54,660
AUDIENCE: The fifth one as well because the learning rate should

283
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:57,540
be very, very small, otherwise we're just going to diverge.

284
00:12:57,540 --> 00:13:00,480
SARA BEERY: So this one in the center of the bottom,

285
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:04,160
this is also difficult because the gradient is very steep.

286
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,780
It's very, very high as you get close to the minimum.

287
00:13:06,780 --> 00:13:09,420
And so the challenge there is that as you

288
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,000
get close to that minimum, the gradient is really large,

289
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,580
so then it's going to bounce you somewhere really far away.

290
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:15,940
So essentially, it's really difficult

291
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:17,607
to actually ever hit the minimum because

292
00:13:17,607 --> 00:13:22,220
of what we call exploding gradients, yeah, so definitely.

293
00:13:22,220 --> 00:13:27,960
And one nice intuition around easy versus hard to optimize,

294
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:30,800
you can think about flowing water.

295
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:34,300
So in this example over here, you would actually,

296
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:36,290
no matter where you started, you would actually

297
00:13:36,290 --> 00:13:39,650
end up getting to that minimum because, essentially, you

298
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:41,250
can think of water flowing.

299
00:13:41,250 --> 00:13:44,170
No matter where it came from, it would still get to that middle.

300
00:13:44,170 --> 00:13:47,610
Of course, that really only helps you thinking about,

301
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:49,015
is it minimizing?

302
00:13:49,015 --> 00:13:50,890
So that really is only filtering out this one

303
00:13:50,890 --> 00:13:53,890
that has multiple minima.

304
00:13:53,890 --> 00:14:01,570
So one thing that's true is that most existing popular neural

305
00:14:01,570 --> 00:14:06,910
network components will exhibit certain properties.

306
00:14:06,910 --> 00:14:09,950
So like we said, these would be hard to optimize.

307
00:14:09,950 --> 00:14:12,610
But now I want to go through what these different landscapes

308
00:14:12,610 --> 00:14:14,888
would look like, the pros and cons of them.

309
00:14:14,888 --> 00:14:16,930
And then we'll get into some high-level intuition

310
00:14:16,930 --> 00:14:18,430
and talk about where the field seems

311
00:14:18,430 --> 00:14:21,130
to be going in terms of the types of loss

312
00:14:21,130 --> 00:14:22,810
that we're calculating.

313
00:14:22,810 --> 00:14:26,097
So something like this, this is like maybe the simplest case.

314
00:14:26,097 --> 00:14:27,930
This is a case that if you're a theoretician

315
00:14:27,930 --> 00:14:31,250
is really great because you can assume convexity.

316
00:14:31,250 --> 00:14:32,682
You have a single minimum.

317
00:14:32,682 --> 00:14:34,390
All the gradients point to it everywhere.

318
00:14:34,390 --> 00:14:36,330
And the gradient will gracefully go to 0

319
00:14:36,330 --> 00:14:38,970
as the minimum is approached.

320
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:41,850
Here, we have discontinuity, but it's well

321
00:14:41,850 --> 00:14:44,130
defined in terms of the derivatives

322
00:14:44,130 --> 00:14:45,910
on both sides of that discontinuity.

323
00:14:45,910 --> 00:14:49,330
And this is not a problem at all for PyTorch.

324
00:14:49,330 --> 00:14:52,190
Here, even if this is not completely flat,

325
00:14:52,190 --> 00:14:54,890
which you can see here it isn't, it's

326
00:14:54,890 --> 00:14:56,870
what we call a vanishing gradient.

327
00:14:56,870 --> 00:15:01,470
The progress would be really slow, and noise in your batches,

328
00:15:01,470 --> 00:15:04,370
for example, might end up dominating over the signal.

329
00:15:04,370 --> 00:15:07,250
So this would be very difficult to optimize.

330
00:15:07,250 --> 00:15:10,530
Here, this one, just zero gradient everywhere, basically,

331
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:13,330
completely uninformative as how to make progress,

332
00:15:13,330 --> 00:15:16,710
and so it's really difficult to ever hit any low loss region.

333
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:17,830
There was a question?

334
00:15:17,830 --> 00:15:18,330
Yeah?

335
00:15:18,330 --> 00:15:21,250
AUDIENCE: Can you take a second to explain the graphs that

336
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:22,230
are on the right side.

337
00:15:22,230 --> 00:15:23,570
SARA BEERY: Oh, yeah, sorry.

338
00:15:23,570 --> 00:15:25,950
Sometimes I assume something is intuitive, and it's not.

339
00:15:25,950 --> 00:15:28,170
So thank you so much for asking.

340
00:15:28,170 --> 00:15:32,960
So this is for different parameters.

341
00:15:32,960 --> 00:15:36,240
All of these are single versions of parameters.

342
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:40,800
So this is now looking at that plot.

343
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:45,720
Now here, this is some optimizer steps going from 0 to 100,

344
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:47,800
so assuming you're starting from the top.

345
00:15:47,800 --> 00:15:50,360
And it's basically stepping through,

346
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:52,000
assuming this was your loss landscape,

347
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,920
and you had some of standard learning rate,

348
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,160
where the model would actually move

349
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,180
throughout gradient descent, stochastic gradient descent.

350
00:16:02,180 --> 00:16:03,740
So in this case, there's no signal.

351
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:05,320
It just doesn't go anywhere.

352
00:16:05,320 --> 00:16:07,600
Whereas in the previous slide or this one,

353
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:09,800
for example, you might start out over here.

354
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:12,100
And it might bounce across and then go back and forth,

355
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:15,360
but It will actually, eventually, find that minimum.

356
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:16,380
Thanks for the question.

357
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:18,640
It was a good one.

358
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:20,880
Yeah, so zero gradient-- this is what we were talking

359
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:22,920
about in terms of instability.

360
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:25,260
This one has what we call an exploding gradient.

361
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:29,360
The gradient goes to infinity as the minimizer is approached,

362
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:31,120
so you have really unstable updates,

363
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:32,460
and you can really overshoot.

364
00:16:32,460 --> 00:16:35,063
It can be very difficult to ever actually hit that minimum.

365
00:16:35,063 --> 00:16:36,480
And then, here, when we're talking

366
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:38,920
about multiple local minima, where you initialize

367
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:42,240
matters a lot.

368
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:45,080
One way that people handle the fact that many of our lost

369
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:47,340
landscapes are not guaranteed to be convex.

370
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:50,040
We're not guaranteed that they only have a single local minima.

371
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:54,640
This is why you might, for example, try experimentally

372
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:56,240
a bunch of different random seeds,

373
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:59,480
and try to then select a model that ends up looking better.

374
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:01,043
And the variance in your performance,

375
00:17:01,043 --> 00:17:02,960
based on different random seeds, will tell you

376
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:08,160
something about how unstable your loss landscape is.

377
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:11,020
So there's a couple things that we wanted to bring up.

378
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:11,520
Yeah?

379
00:17:11,520 --> 00:17:15,002
AUDIENCE: Question about-- you said that PyTorch [INAUDIBLE].

380
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:22,859
SARA BEERY: Well, PyTorch can make anything differentiable.

381
00:17:22,859 --> 00:17:25,599
That doesn't mean it will be easy to optimize.

382
00:17:25,599 --> 00:17:27,335
So for example, that one that's flat,

383
00:17:27,335 --> 00:17:29,710
it's differentiable in PyTorch, but that doesn't actually

384
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:32,150
mean that it's going to find a local minimum because it doesn't

385
00:17:32,150 --> 00:17:32,830
exist.

386
00:17:32,830 --> 00:17:37,170
So it's computationally possible to calculate a gradient there,

387
00:17:37,170 --> 00:17:39,110
but the gradient will still be 0.

388
00:17:39,110 --> 00:17:40,762
So essentially, PyTorch and autograd,

389
00:17:40,762 --> 00:17:42,470
they mean that even if you take something

390
00:17:42,470 --> 00:17:44,690
that would traditionally be non-differentiable,

391
00:17:44,690 --> 00:17:47,190
you can still calculate derivatives in PyTorch.

392
00:17:47,190 --> 00:17:49,190
So we assume things like directional derivatives

393
00:17:49,190 --> 00:17:51,750
around discontinuities, but that doesn't actually

394
00:17:51,750 --> 00:17:54,177
mean that every loss landscape is equal.

395
00:17:54,177 --> 00:17:56,010
That's kind of what we're getting into here,

396
00:17:56,010 --> 00:17:59,550
is these ideas around what makes a good versus a bad loss

397
00:17:59,550 --> 00:18:00,290
landscape.

398
00:18:00,290 --> 00:18:01,910
Does that make sense?

399
00:18:01,910 --> 00:18:02,710
Cool.

400
00:18:02,710 --> 00:18:03,210
OK.

401
00:18:03,210 --> 00:18:04,835
So one thing that we wanted to bring up

402
00:18:04,835 --> 00:18:07,790
was this idea of an evolution strategy.

403
00:18:07,790 --> 00:18:09,990
So these are gradient like.

404
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:12,590
They find locally loss-minimizing directions

405
00:18:12,590 --> 00:18:14,350
in parameter space.

406
00:18:14,350 --> 00:18:15,910
And the way that they do that is they

407
00:18:15,910 --> 00:18:19,790
sample small perturbations around your existing parameter

408
00:18:19,790 --> 00:18:22,110
values and then move towards the perturbations

409
00:18:22,110 --> 00:18:24,790
that achieve lower loss.

410
00:18:24,790 --> 00:18:29,630
So for example, you could take some perturbation or error

411
00:18:29,630 --> 00:18:33,670
that's normally distributed.

412
00:18:33,670 --> 00:18:37,870
And then when you're actually calculating your values,

413
00:18:37,870 --> 00:18:39,750
you calculate them of the loss function

414
00:18:39,750 --> 00:18:44,750
plus some amount of perturbation in that normal space.

415
00:18:44,750 --> 00:18:46,430
And then when you're doing your updates,

416
00:18:46,430 --> 00:18:51,710
now you have this almost robustness that comes in.

417
00:18:51,710 --> 00:18:56,350
And it will actually select successfully minimize, even

418
00:18:56,350 --> 00:18:58,430
something that looks like this.

419
00:18:58,430 --> 00:19:00,830
So here, even if we started up here

420
00:19:00,830 --> 00:19:06,830
in this non-minimal situation, because we're

421
00:19:06,830 --> 00:19:08,490
perturbing the values of the function,

422
00:19:08,490 --> 00:19:11,270
it might bounce us over into a better value,

423
00:19:11,270 --> 00:19:13,330
just by sampling this cost function,

424
00:19:13,330 --> 00:19:15,350
these different things.

425
00:19:15,350 --> 00:19:18,030
So this is something, these evolution strategies

426
00:19:18,030 --> 00:19:20,750
is something that sometimes people will take into account.

427
00:19:20,750 --> 00:19:22,350
And then another important one is

428
00:19:22,350 --> 00:19:24,710
this idea of gradient clipping.

429
00:19:24,710 --> 00:19:26,620
And this is a mechanism that helps us

430
00:19:26,620 --> 00:19:30,520
with, basically, peakiness or pointiness in our loss function.

431
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:32,820
So we'll successfully minimize this function.

432
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:34,680
And so, here, it's quite simple.

433
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:38,140
If the gradient gets too high above some large value,

434
00:19:38,140 --> 00:19:40,080
you just scale them back to that value.

435
00:19:40,080 --> 00:19:42,660
So you essentially just put a clipping filter

436
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:44,380
on top of your gradient.

437
00:19:44,380 --> 00:19:48,085
And this is a useful and commonly used hack.

438
00:19:48,085 --> 00:19:49,460
So here, essentially, what you do

439
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:53,120
is you just calculate the change in your parameters.

440
00:19:53,120 --> 00:19:57,100
And then you move in the direction of that maximal change

441
00:19:57,100 --> 00:20:00,120
but clipped to some maximum value.

442
00:20:00,120 --> 00:20:03,700
So you won't let your model move too far in any direction.

443
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:07,100
And this, then, helps us with not oscillating back and forth

444
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:09,100
across something.

445
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:13,220
So what then is important in a loss function?

446
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:16,700
So a few things that are consistently showing up--

447
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:18,380
one-- though this is, of course, not

448
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:21,740
required-- everywhere continuous, another one,

449
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:23,240
everywhere differentiable.

450
00:20:23,240 --> 00:20:26,260
And the third one would be everywhere smooth.

451
00:20:26,260 --> 00:20:32,260
So one of the most commonly used non-linearities is ReLU.

452
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:37,060
And this is something that is everywhere continuous,

453
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:39,120
almost everywhere differentiable,

454
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:41,140
but it's not everywhere smooth.

455
00:20:41,140 --> 00:20:47,460
We have this kink in it.

456
00:20:47,460 --> 00:20:53,220
Now, recently, there's been a lot of work

457
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:56,540
that will instead use something like a ReLU, which is

458
00:20:56,540 --> 00:21:00,820
a Gaussian error linear unit.

459
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:04,540
And this one, essentially, just takes the parameters times

460
00:21:04,540 --> 00:21:06,207
phi of the parameters, where this

461
00:21:06,207 --> 00:21:08,540
is the cumulative distribution function for the Gaussian

462
00:21:08,540 --> 00:21:09,520
distribution.

463
00:21:09,520 --> 00:21:12,540
It looks something like this in two dimensions.

464
00:21:12,540 --> 00:21:14,440
And this one is everywhere continuous,

465
00:21:14,440 --> 00:21:17,347
everywhere differentiable, and everywhere smooth.

466
00:21:17,347 --> 00:21:19,180
The important thing I want to point out here

467
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:22,930
is that we can agree that these three things might

468
00:21:22,930 --> 00:21:24,650
make optimization easier.

469
00:21:24,650 --> 00:21:28,170
And even if we don't precisely know experimentally

470
00:21:28,170 --> 00:21:29,770
or theoretically, which properties

471
00:21:29,770 --> 00:21:33,190
are needed for training neural networks,

472
00:21:33,190 --> 00:21:35,370
trends do seem to be moving towards functions

473
00:21:35,370 --> 00:21:39,330
which satisfy all three, so things like this GeLU.

474
00:21:39,330 --> 00:21:42,010
Any questions about optimization and SGD?

475
00:21:42,010 --> 00:21:42,810
Yeah?

476
00:21:42,810 --> 00:21:46,970
AUDIENCE: What was the [INAUDIBLE], use that for again?

477
00:21:46,970 --> 00:21:49,685
SARA BEERY: So evolution strategies?

478
00:21:49,685 --> 00:21:50,310
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

479
00:21:50,310 --> 00:21:53,330
SARA BEERY: Essentially, instead of always moving

480
00:21:53,330 --> 00:21:54,930
in the direction of the gradient,

481
00:21:54,930 --> 00:22:00,150
you just randomly sample around your current parameter values,

482
00:22:00,150 --> 00:22:03,930
and then you move in the random direction that maximizes

483
00:22:03,930 --> 00:22:06,770
or that minimizes your cost.

484
00:22:06,770 --> 00:22:09,770
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

485
00:22:09,770 --> 00:22:12,210
SARA BEERY: Essentially, it's helping you maybe handle

486
00:22:12,210 --> 00:22:15,250
things like vanishing gradients or some

487
00:22:15,250 --> 00:22:18,570
of these parts of your loss landscape

488
00:22:18,570 --> 00:22:21,250
that might be not actually functional

489
00:22:21,250 --> 00:22:23,410
to optimize super efficiently.

490
00:22:23,410 --> 00:22:26,210
And I think you can almost think of it--

491
00:22:26,210 --> 00:22:31,137
one intuition, It's almost like regularization in your loss,

492
00:22:31,137 --> 00:22:31,970
if that makes sense.

493
00:22:31,970 --> 00:22:33,690
AUDIENCE: Also, follow up, how common

494
00:22:33,690 --> 00:22:37,850
is it that people use everything together?

495
00:22:37,850 --> 00:22:43,450
SARA BEERY: Yeah, I mean, I think

496
00:22:43,450 --> 00:22:48,050
everything's a bit of a big, soup pot these days.

497
00:22:48,050 --> 00:22:50,890
There's a lot of these different experimental tricks.

498
00:22:50,890 --> 00:22:53,810
Some of them, even if they seem quite different,

499
00:22:53,810 --> 00:22:56,010
almost seem like they achieve the same ends, when

500
00:22:56,010 --> 00:22:59,730
you talk about the experimental performance.

501
00:22:59,730 --> 00:23:03,630
I would say that a lot of the really top-performing,

502
00:23:03,630 --> 00:23:07,450
state-of-the-art models on different types of benchmarks

503
00:23:07,450 --> 00:23:10,210
are often ones that incorporate a lot of these tricks,

504
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:16,570
different hacks and mechanisms to try to get a little better

505
00:23:16,570 --> 00:23:19,680
at finding some maybe slightly better optimum,

506
00:23:19,680 --> 00:23:22,680
but I don't think we actually have a really great recipe

507
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:26,400
for given a certain data set, given a certain architecture,

508
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:30,520
what is the right way to optimize that architecture?

509
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:31,340
Yeah?

510
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:32,840
AUDIENCE: Can you please explain how

511
00:23:32,840 --> 00:23:38,520
ReLU and GeLU relates to this question of [INAUDIBLE]?

512
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:40,760
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so, again, I'm not

513
00:23:40,760 --> 00:23:44,240
I'm not saying here that GeLU is much better than ReLU

514
00:23:44,240 --> 00:23:47,200
all the time because that's not necessarily true.

515
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:51,520
It's just pointing out that the trend in these different loss

516
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:53,440
terms for neural network training

517
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:57,920
does at least seem to be moving away from functions that don't

518
00:23:57,920 --> 00:23:59,680
satisfy all three of these.

519
00:23:59,680 --> 00:24:02,000
But again, we don't necessarily know,

520
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:03,960
or we haven't decided as a community

521
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:09,000
or even gotten close to some theoretical guarantee

522
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:14,280
as a community as to what is the optimal mechanism.

523
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:15,600
Yeah?

524
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:17,960
AUDIENCE: Are these loss functions?

