600
00:27:17,260 --> 00:27:18,677
So I'm going to move on to talking

601
00:27:18,677 --> 00:27:20,540
about computation graphs.

602
00:27:20,540 --> 00:27:23,660
So we're going to move to thinking of this

603
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:26,600
all from this perspective of differential programming.

604
00:27:26,600 --> 00:27:31,660
Essentially, here, we're talking about computation graph.

605
00:27:31,660 --> 00:27:34,500
Here, this is something like a graph of different functional

606
00:27:34,500 --> 00:27:36,115
transformations.

607
00:27:36,115 --> 00:27:37,740
Each of those functional transformation

608
00:27:37,740 --> 00:27:39,420
is a node in the graph.

609
00:27:39,420 --> 00:27:41,580
And then when you string all of those together,

610
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:45,020
you can perform some useful computation.

611
00:27:45,020 --> 00:27:49,600
So intuitively, maybe this is some sort of decision tree

612
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:52,900
that's a very simple version of a graph

613
00:27:52,900 --> 00:27:54,660
of functional transformations.

614
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:56,940
So maybe I'm saying, all right, I'm

615
00:27:56,940 --> 00:27:59,860
going to try to make it to the classroom.

616
00:27:59,860 --> 00:28:04,740
So I'm going to go into a building.

617
00:28:04,740 --> 00:28:07,620
And then maybe on the right, you've

618
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:09,643
gone into building 45, which is correct.

619
00:28:09,643 --> 00:28:11,560
And on the left, you've gone into building 32,

620
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:13,060
which is incorrect.

621
00:28:13,060 --> 00:28:15,620
And then there's these different decisions

622
00:28:15,620 --> 00:28:18,860
you might make based on the values of your input

623
00:28:18,860 --> 00:28:19,940
at any given time.

624
00:28:19,940 --> 00:28:21,980
So if you did go into building 45,

625
00:28:21,980 --> 00:28:26,580
then you could maybe take the elevator or take the stairs.

626
00:28:26,580 --> 00:28:29,220
And that decision over what action you'd take

627
00:28:29,220 --> 00:28:31,483
could be a really, really simple heuristic version

628
00:28:31,483 --> 00:28:33,900
of a functional transformation because you're transforming

629
00:28:33,900 --> 00:28:36,740
your inputs to some output.

630
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:42,580
Of course, what we actually see here

631
00:28:42,580 --> 00:28:45,420
when we think about machine learning systems is each

632
00:28:45,420 --> 00:28:47,940
of these maybe blocks are something

633
00:28:47,940 --> 00:28:50,860
like a layer in a neural network or even

634
00:28:50,860 --> 00:28:53,340
an entire neural network.

635
00:28:53,340 --> 00:28:57,820
And there's nothing that says what the size of these

636
00:28:57,820 --> 00:29:01,420
are, any of restrictions when you're thinking about it really

637
00:29:01,420 --> 00:29:05,460
from this high-level perspective of a computational graph.

638
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:08,910
So deep learning primarily deals with DAGs.

639
00:29:08,910 --> 00:29:10,950
These are directed acyclic graph.

640
00:29:10,950 --> 00:29:12,690
So directed means the information only

641
00:29:12,690 --> 00:29:15,290
goes one direction on any given edge.

642
00:29:15,290 --> 00:29:19,450
Acyclic means you don't have loops or cycles.

643
00:29:19,450 --> 00:29:22,330
And we also assume that every single one of these nodes

644
00:29:22,330 --> 00:29:23,850
is differentiable.

645
00:29:23,850 --> 00:29:25,650
Though we did just say that using PyTorch,

646
00:29:25,650 --> 00:29:28,230
you can calculate a derivative for almost anything,

647
00:29:28,230 --> 00:29:31,850
so that becomes less of a hard constraint.

648
00:29:31,850 --> 00:29:36,970
So if we now think about this from the perspective

649
00:29:36,970 --> 00:29:40,090
of, for example, a really simple multilayer perceptron,

650
00:29:40,090 --> 00:29:43,170
like we talked about on Thursday.

651
00:29:43,170 --> 00:29:45,290
Here, you can think of that now, again,

652
00:29:45,290 --> 00:29:47,230
as one of these directed acyclic graph.

653
00:29:47,230 --> 00:29:48,610
You have your input x.

654
00:29:48,610 --> 00:29:50,950
You send that through this linear layer,

655
00:29:50,950 --> 00:29:53,250
which is one node in the graph.

656
00:29:53,250 --> 00:29:55,370
Then that gives you some hidden unit z.

657
00:29:55,370 --> 00:29:57,750
You send that through an activation function or ReLU

658
00:29:57,750 --> 00:29:59,890
that's, again, some computation that takes an input

659
00:29:59,890 --> 00:30:02,970
and changes it to create an output.

660
00:30:02,970 --> 00:30:05,845
Now, another hidden layer h, hidden state h.

661
00:30:05,845 --> 00:30:08,470
And then you take that and run it through another linear layer.

662
00:30:08,470 --> 00:30:10,730
And that gives you your output y.

663
00:30:10,730 --> 00:30:12,730
So even an MLP--

664
00:30:12,730 --> 00:30:16,210
easy to represent as a computation graph.

665
00:30:16,210 --> 00:30:20,450
So now let's talk about what a forward pass actually looks like

666
00:30:20,450 --> 00:30:24,810
and what's required to calculate a forward pass.

667
00:30:24,810 --> 00:30:28,230
So in the simplest sense, for any model,

668
00:30:28,230 --> 00:30:32,030
for any set of parameters, for anything within that function,

669
00:30:32,030 --> 00:30:34,050
you take in some input value.

670
00:30:34,050 --> 00:30:35,610
You take in some theta, which are

671
00:30:35,610 --> 00:30:39,930
the parameters of that component or of function.

672
00:30:39,930 --> 00:30:44,410
And then you map through that functional transformation,

673
00:30:44,410 --> 00:30:46,850
the inputs, the parameters, and you get out

674
00:30:46,850 --> 00:30:48,530
the outputs of that layer.

675
00:30:48,530 --> 00:30:54,090
So that's a forward pass for any type of computational function.

676
00:30:54,090 --> 00:30:56,330
Now, you can have multiple layers, right?

677
00:30:56,330 --> 00:30:59,570
This computation graph, for example, could represent an MLP.

678
00:30:59,570 --> 00:31:03,280
Now you have your inputs and your parameters

679
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:04,787
for that first component.

680
00:31:04,787 --> 00:31:05,620
Then, you get those.

681
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:06,520
That's an output.

682
00:31:06,520 --> 00:31:08,540
That becomes the input of the next component,

683
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:10,240
et cetera, et cetera.

684
00:31:10,240 --> 00:31:15,360
And then, at the end, maybe you have something like this loss

685
00:31:15,360 --> 00:31:17,380
that you use to calculate your cost.

686
00:31:17,380 --> 00:31:23,440
So then when you're thinking about learning, now here,

687
00:31:23,440 --> 00:31:25,580
in order to calculate this learning,

688
00:31:25,580 --> 00:31:27,280
that's where we think about computing

689
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:30,120
the gradients of the cost with respect

690
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:33,680
to all of those model parameters that happen all

691
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:35,100
the way through the network.

692
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:37,960
And so by design, every single layer

693
00:31:37,960 --> 00:31:41,000
will be differentiable with respect to its inputs

694
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,420
and, therefore, this is actually possible to compute.

695
00:31:43,420 --> 00:31:46,560
It is possible to compute that gradient with respect

696
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:48,020
to all of these model parameters.

697
00:31:48,020 --> 00:31:49,680
So you can figure out how you need

698
00:31:49,680 --> 00:31:55,640
to update those model parameters to move to a more optimal cost.

699
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:56,440
OK.

700
00:31:56,440 --> 00:32:00,320
So a bit of an aside, just in case any of you

701
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:05,360
are not brushed up on your matrix calculus

702
00:32:05,360 --> 00:32:07,495
because this will show up a lot.

703
00:32:07,495 --> 00:32:09,120
We're going to be doing a lot of matrix

704
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:10,622
multiplications in this class.

705
00:32:10,622 --> 00:32:12,580
Hopefully, this is super review for all of you,

706
00:32:12,580 --> 00:32:14,360
but just a reminder.

707
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:18,240
So we're going to assume that our inputs are, for example,

708
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:20,960
column vectors of size n by 1.

709
00:32:20,960 --> 00:32:25,920
Now if we define a function on that vector, y equals f of x.

710
00:32:25,920 --> 00:32:29,600
If y is a scalar, then the derivative

711
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:33,560
of that output with respect to x, the input,

712
00:32:33,560 --> 00:32:37,640
would be a horizontal vector, where

713
00:32:37,640 --> 00:32:39,680
for each element of that vector, you're

714
00:32:39,680 --> 00:32:42,760
getting the partial derivative of y

715
00:32:42,760 --> 00:32:45,960
with respect to that first element, or second element,

716
00:32:45,960 --> 00:32:47,200
or third element of x.

717
00:32:47,200 --> 00:32:53,200
And then that is a row vector of size 1 by n.

718
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:57,120
If y is a vector of n by 1, then we

719
00:32:57,120 --> 00:33:01,870
assume the Jacobian formulation of the partial derivative of y

720
00:33:01,870 --> 00:33:05,750
with respect to x, where now you, essentially, end up

721
00:33:05,750 --> 00:33:08,270
with something that's a matrix of size m by n,

722
00:33:08,270 --> 00:33:11,950
so m rows, which is the size of y, by n columns

723
00:33:11,950 --> 00:33:12,990
is the size of x.

724
00:33:12,990 --> 00:33:15,430
And each element of that matrix will

725
00:33:15,430 --> 00:33:18,150
be the partial derivative of that element of y

726
00:33:18,150 --> 00:33:21,430
with that element of x,

727
00:33:21,430 --> 00:33:25,530
Now if y is a scalar and x is a matrix--

728
00:33:25,530 --> 00:33:29,510
so, for example, an image of size n by m--

729
00:33:29,510 --> 00:33:33,470
then the partial derivative of y with respect to that matrix

730
00:33:33,470 --> 00:33:36,350
x will be a matrix of size m by n,

731
00:33:36,350 --> 00:33:39,830
where now here, each component corresponds

732
00:33:39,830 --> 00:33:43,590
to that relevant component of x.

733
00:33:43,590 --> 00:33:45,710
And note that by taking that derivative,

734
00:33:45,710 --> 00:33:48,550
we flipped the rows and columns.

735
00:33:48,550 --> 00:33:50,870
So it's been transposed.

736
00:33:50,870 --> 00:33:54,037
And according to Wikipedia, the three types

737
00:33:54,037 --> 00:33:55,870
of derivatives that have not been considered

738
00:33:55,870 --> 00:33:59,250
are those involving vectors by matrices, matrices by vectors,

739
00:33:59,250 --> 00:34:01,350
and matrices by matrices.

740
00:34:01,350 --> 00:34:03,310
Notation is not widely agreed upon.

741
00:34:03,310 --> 00:34:06,630
We're now going to see any of in this class.

742
00:34:06,630 --> 00:34:09,830
Cool, so chain rule.

743
00:34:09,830 --> 00:34:13,710
For the function h of x is f of g of x,

744
00:34:13,710 --> 00:34:15,630
how do you take the derivative?

745
00:34:15,630 --> 00:34:18,150
Who remembers the chain rule?

746
00:34:18,150 --> 00:34:19,590
Yeah?

