00:34:30,983 --> 00:34:33,150
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

752
00:34:33,150 --> 00:34:34,810
SARA BEERY: I don't think that's right.

753
00:34:34,810 --> 00:34:38,010
[LAUGHS] It's all right, man.

754
00:34:38,010 --> 00:34:38,730
It's all right.

755
00:34:38,730 --> 00:34:39,230
[LAUGHS]

756
00:34:39,230 --> 00:34:42,030
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

757
00:34:42,030 --> 00:34:42,710
SARA BEERY: OK.

758
00:34:42,710 --> 00:34:45,150
Go for it.

759
00:34:45,150 --> 00:34:49,750
AUDIENCE: g x prime with the f prime of gx.

760
00:34:49,750 --> 00:34:50,670
SARA BEERY: Yes.

761
00:34:50,670 --> 00:34:53,510
So the derivative of x f with respect

762
00:34:53,510 --> 00:34:58,500
to g of x times the derivative of g with respect to x, yeah.

763
00:34:58,500 --> 00:34:59,700
Good job, dude.

764
00:34:59,700 --> 00:35:00,710
Way to go for it.

765
00:35:00,710 --> 00:35:02,460
No one's going to judge anyone for getting

766
00:35:02,460 --> 00:35:03,960
something wrong in this class.

767
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:07,460
[LAUGHS, APPLAUSE]

768
00:35:07,460 --> 00:35:09,780
All right, so then, actually, if we write this out

769
00:35:09,780 --> 00:35:13,380
as z is f of u and u is g of x, then

770
00:35:13,380 --> 00:35:15,940
you can write it out this way.

771
00:35:15,940 --> 00:35:17,580
So essentially, you can write this out

772
00:35:17,580 --> 00:35:20,740
as the multiplication of two partial derivatives.

773
00:35:20,740 --> 00:35:26,980
So that means that p would be the length of vector u.

774
00:35:26,980 --> 00:35:33,100
And m would be-- wait, what the fuck is p?

775
00:35:33,100 --> 00:35:34,200
What am I talking about?

