790
00:36:34,780 --> 00:36:38,380
p is the length of vector u equals the size of u

791
00:36:38,380 --> 00:36:39,980
is confusing.

792
00:36:39,980 --> 00:36:41,200
But, yes, m by p.

793
00:36:41,200 --> 00:36:44,604
And then what's the last one?

794
00:36:44,604 --> 00:36:45,816
AUDIENCE: p by n.

795
00:36:45,816 --> 00:36:48,200
SARA BEERY: p by n, exactly Cool.

796
00:36:48,200 --> 00:36:53,090
So therefore, if you said the size of z is 1, the size of u

797
00:36:53,090 --> 00:36:57,090
is 2, the size of x is 4, then essentially what you're saying

798
00:36:57,090 --> 00:37:00,890
is this derivative would be, essentially, equal

799
00:37:00,890 --> 00:37:06,690
to this product of two matrices, though one is really a vector.

800
00:37:06,690 --> 00:37:07,930
All right.

801
00:37:07,930 --> 00:37:09,530
We're bringing this up because we

802
00:37:09,530 --> 00:37:14,010
will see a lot of matrices times matrices in the next bit.

803
00:37:14,010 --> 00:37:17,970
So that's the end of our little bit of a vector calculus

804
00:37:17,970 --> 00:37:20,730
or matrix calculus reminder.

805
00:37:20,730 --> 00:37:23,450
So let's talk about backprop.

806
00:37:23,450 --> 00:37:25,230
How does any of this become possible?

807
00:37:25,230 --> 00:37:28,730
How do we calculate the update in those parameters?

808
00:37:28,730 --> 00:37:30,690
Essentially, what we need to calculate

809
00:37:30,690 --> 00:37:34,170
is the partial derivative of j with respect

810
00:37:34,170 --> 00:37:41,050
to, for example, those initial layers in the model, theta 1.

811
00:37:41,050 --> 00:37:47,010
So by the chain rule, you can essentially factor it out.

812
00:37:47,010 --> 00:37:51,330
And you can map that back through all

813
00:37:51,330 --> 00:37:55,850
of these different layers, so the inputs and outputs

814
00:37:55,850 --> 00:37:59,090
of x as it goes through all these different layers.

815
00:37:59,090 --> 00:38:02,490
And if you need to calculate the partial derivative of j

816
00:38:02,490 --> 00:38:06,570
with respect to that second layer's parameters,

817
00:38:06,570 --> 00:38:10,650
you'll note that it's the same essentially.

818
00:38:10,650 --> 00:38:13,847
But the thing that's different is, here, these last terms

819
00:38:13,847 --> 00:38:14,430
are different.

820
00:38:14,430 --> 00:38:17,570
But everything for those two are shared.

821
00:38:17,570 --> 00:38:20,010
And this is really the trick, right?

822
00:38:20,010 --> 00:38:22,850
We could separately compute all of the derivatives using

823
00:38:22,850 --> 00:38:25,650
the chain rule, but because these terms in the gray box

824
00:38:25,650 --> 00:38:28,130
are shared, we only need to compute them once.

825
00:38:28,130 --> 00:38:30,770
So back propagation is a pretty simple algorithm

826
00:38:30,770 --> 00:38:34,410
for propagating shared terms through the computation graph.

827
00:38:34,410 --> 00:38:35,900
It's basically an efficiency trick,

828
00:38:35,900 --> 00:38:37,650
but it's one that makes it computationally

829
00:38:37,650 --> 00:38:41,250
practical for very large models to be able to actually calculate

830
00:38:41,250 --> 00:38:43,130
these gradients.

831
00:38:43,130 --> 00:38:46,970
So if this is a forward pass, we're sending data forward

832
00:38:46,970 --> 00:38:49,640
through the network, and then computing the outputs

833
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:52,320
and calculating the overall loss.

834
00:38:52,320 --> 00:38:54,960
Then a backwards pass look like this,

835
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:58,960
which is that we send our error signal, the gradients, backwards

836
00:38:58,960 --> 00:39:01,460
through the network from the outputs,

837
00:39:01,460 --> 00:39:05,560
and the loss back to the inputs and the parameters.

838
00:39:05,560 --> 00:39:07,320
Does this make sense?

839
00:39:07,320 --> 00:39:08,800
Cool.

840
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:12,280
So what is a backwards pass look like for a generic layer?

841
00:39:12,280 --> 00:39:14,600
We're going to keep track of two kinds of arrays

842
00:39:14,600 --> 00:39:16,320
of partial derivatives here.

843
00:39:16,320 --> 00:39:20,840
So first, L is the gradient of the layer outputs with regard

844
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:21,972
to the layer inputs.

845
00:39:21,972 --> 00:39:23,180
This will be a matrix, right?

846
00:39:23,180 --> 00:39:26,560
This is, what way does each of these parameters

847
00:39:26,560 --> 00:39:30,800
need to change to move in the maximal direction

848
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:32,980
of optimization?

849
00:39:39,640 --> 00:39:41,480
So here, this is essentially a matrix

850
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:46,640
that's just telling you which would

851
00:39:46,640 --> 00:39:50,240
these parameters optimally move.

852
00:39:50,240 --> 00:39:52,660
And then second, we're going to keep track of g.

853
00:39:52,660 --> 00:39:55,240
And this is the gradient of the cost with respect

854
00:39:55,240 --> 00:39:56,260
to the activations.

855
00:39:56,260 --> 00:39:58,280
And this will be a row vector.

856
00:39:58,280 --> 00:40:03,160
So this is basically saying, yeah, essentially,

857
00:40:03,160 --> 00:40:04,640
what's the partial derivative of j

858
00:40:04,640 --> 00:40:08,040
with respect to x, with respect to that input?

859
00:40:08,040 --> 00:40:10,040
So now, if we want to update the parameters,

860
00:40:10,040 --> 00:40:14,080
that's actually really easy if we have both L and g.

861
00:40:14,080 --> 00:40:16,740
Essentially, it's just a multiplication, right?

862
00:40:16,740 --> 00:40:19,800
You have g out, and you multiply that by L of theta.

863
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:21,680
And that matrix multiplication actually

864
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:23,000
just updates your layer.

865
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:25,440
That tells you which way to move.

866
00:40:25,440 --> 00:40:27,440
And so now you take your parameters,

867
00:40:27,440 --> 00:40:29,240
and you change them by your learning

868
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:33,320
rate multiplied by that update.

869
00:40:33,320 --> 00:40:36,520
So how do we actually get L and g for each layer?

870
00:40:36,520 --> 00:40:39,160
So L comes from that derivative function of the layer, which

871
00:40:39,160 --> 00:40:40,540
we assume is provided.

872
00:40:40,540 --> 00:40:43,420
We assume for any given layer, that it is differentiable,

873
00:40:43,420 --> 00:40:46,440
and that we have a function that lets us take that derivative.

874
00:40:46,440 --> 00:40:50,160
And then g is something that can be computed iteratively

875
00:40:50,160 --> 00:40:51,340
via recurrence.

876
00:40:51,340 --> 00:40:55,440
So the input gradient will always

877
00:40:55,440 --> 00:41:01,543
be equal to the output gradient times that L for that layer.

878
00:41:01,543 --> 00:41:03,960
So this is, essentially, the back propagation of the error

879
00:41:03,960 --> 00:41:07,000
signal back through the model.

880
00:41:07,000 --> 00:41:09,480
And then all of this machinery is

881
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:12,420
used to then compute parameter update directions,

882
00:41:12,420 --> 00:41:15,520
so which way do we need to go?

883
00:41:15,520 --> 00:41:18,720
So the full algorithm-- forward then backward.

884
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:20,880
First, we're moving forward.

885
00:41:20,880 --> 00:41:22,880
Then we go backward.

886
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:26,185
And then we update and repeat.

887
00:41:26,185 --> 00:41:28,560
And this is, essentially, how you train a neural network,

888
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:29,060
right?

889
00:41:29,060 --> 00:41:31,300
You do a forward pass, calculate loss.

890
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:33,480
You take that loss, propagate it backward,

891
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:36,120
update all your parameters, and do it again,

892
00:41:36,120 --> 00:41:41,320
and again, and again, until you found some likelihood that

893
00:41:41,320 --> 00:41:44,990
makes you think that your model is finished training.

894
00:41:44,990 --> 00:41:47,870
Often, we'll do this-- we'll talk more about mechanisms

895
00:41:47,870 --> 00:41:49,870
for this later, but it's often using something

896
00:41:49,870 --> 00:41:52,510
like a validation set and looking

897
00:41:52,510 --> 00:41:59,750
for some sort of plateauing of change on that validation set.

898
00:41:59,750 --> 00:42:04,590
So if we're doing back propagation,

899
00:42:04,590 --> 00:42:08,070
now what we've got here, in the next set of slides,

900
00:42:08,070 --> 00:42:14,750
are essentially cheat sheets for a lot of this stuff.

901
00:42:14,750 --> 00:42:17,290
It's really just intended to be a pretty nice resource

902
00:42:17,290 --> 00:42:19,870
if you need to refer back and think about how you might need

903
00:42:19,870 --> 00:42:21,590
to implement some of these.

904
00:42:21,590 --> 00:42:27,070
So now a forward pass through some hidden layer.

905
00:42:27,070 --> 00:42:30,350
So now we have the layer before and the layer after.

906
00:42:30,350 --> 00:42:31,550
We take this input.

907
00:42:31,550 --> 00:42:35,750
We run it through the function, and then we have this output.

908
00:42:35,750 --> 00:42:38,230
Now when we're doing that backwards pass,

909
00:42:38,230 --> 00:42:41,670
we're taking the derivative of with respect to the output.

910
00:42:41,670 --> 00:42:45,630
We take the partial derivative with respect to the input.

911
00:42:45,630 --> 00:42:49,970
And then we also are calculating how

912
00:42:49,970 --> 00:42:52,390
we're taking the parameters in, and then we're

913
00:42:52,390 --> 00:42:55,430
calculating the change to those parameters going out.

914
00:42:55,430 --> 00:43:00,390
So this means that layer L for this explicit layer--

915
00:43:00,390 --> 00:43:04,250
so all the inputs are going in with the full lines.

916
00:43:04,250 --> 00:43:08,350
And the outputs are always in the dotted lines here.

917
00:43:08,350 --> 00:43:13,950
So the inputs are then the previous layers output,

918
00:43:13,950 --> 00:43:21,230
the change in the parameters with respect to the next layer,

919
00:43:21,230 --> 00:43:23,630
and the current parameters.

920
00:43:23,630 --> 00:43:26,470
And then the three outputs of every layer

921
00:43:26,470 --> 00:43:30,922
are going to be the output running through the function,

922
00:43:30,922 --> 00:43:32,630
the change in the parameters with respect

923
00:43:32,630 --> 00:43:39,210
to the previous layer, and the actual update to the parameters.

924
00:43:39,210 --> 00:43:40,740
So given those inputs, we just need

925
00:43:40,740 --> 00:43:43,460
to evaluate those three things.

926
00:43:43,460 --> 00:43:47,300
And this actually makes training happen.

927
00:43:47,300 --> 00:43:50,220
So now if we take a summary-- forward pass--

928
00:43:50,220 --> 00:43:52,420
we'll compute the outputs for all the layers.

929
00:43:52,420 --> 00:43:55,100
Backward pass-- we compute the loss derivatives iteratively

930
00:43:55,100 --> 00:43:56,085
from top to bottom.

931
00:43:56,085 --> 00:43:58,460
And then the parameter updates-- we compute the gradients

932
00:43:58,460 --> 00:43:59,627
with respect to the weights.

933
00:43:59,627 --> 00:44:02,820
And then we update all of those weights.

934
00:44:02,820 --> 00:44:07,200
So now what we actually do is we do this over batches.

935
00:44:07,200 --> 00:44:10,042
We're not doing this for a single data point at a time.

936
00:44:10,042 --> 00:44:11,500
And technically, what that means is

937
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:14,460
we want to minimize the average cost over lots

938
00:44:14,460 --> 00:44:17,000
of data points, the entire size of a batch.

939
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:21,980
And in this day and age, with very large memory GPUs,

940
00:44:21,980 --> 00:44:26,100
that batch size could be thousands, thousands of data

941
00:44:26,100 --> 00:44:28,180
points at once.

942
00:44:28,180 --> 00:44:30,900
So then the gradient of the total cost

943
00:44:30,900 --> 00:44:33,860
is just the average of all the gradients of all the

944
00:44:33,860 --> 00:44:34,900
per data point costs.

945
00:44:34,900 --> 00:44:37,320
Because when you take a derivative,

946
00:44:37,320 --> 00:44:41,940
it can move inside the sum, so pretty straightforward.

947
00:44:41,940 --> 00:44:44,733
So now if we actually have this as a linear layer,

948
00:44:44,733 --> 00:44:46,400
let's talk through what that looks like.

949
00:44:46,400 --> 00:44:49,940
Because it turns out everything gets really nice and simple.

950
00:44:49,940 --> 00:44:53,160
So first, forward propagation is quite simple.

951
00:44:53,160 --> 00:44:57,580
If we have a linear layer, the weights of that layer

952
00:44:57,580 --> 00:44:59,500
are just a matrix.

953
00:44:59,500 --> 00:45:01,960
So now if you want to calculate the output.

954
00:45:01,960 --> 00:45:04,220
You just take that matrix and multiply it

955
00:45:04,220 --> 00:45:08,680
by our vector of inputs, so pretty straightforward.

956
00:45:08,680 --> 00:45:12,620
If you want to calculate backpropagation to that input,

957
00:45:12,620 --> 00:45:16,580
this is essentially the input gradient

958
00:45:16,580 --> 00:45:18,940
is going to be the output gradient multiplied

959
00:45:18,940 --> 00:45:21,740
by the update to those parameters with respect

960
00:45:21,740 --> 00:45:22,740
to the input.

961
00:45:22,740 --> 00:45:26,200
So essentially, that just looks like this, right?

962
00:45:26,200 --> 00:45:27,820
This is just that matrix that we've

963
00:45:27,820 --> 00:45:30,620
defined that represents the direction that you would want

964
00:45:30,620 --> 00:45:33,205
to move in for those weights.

965
00:45:33,205 --> 00:45:34,580
And then we look at the component

966
00:45:34,580 --> 00:45:36,792
of the output with respect to the j-th component

967
00:45:36,792 --> 00:45:39,250
of the input, the i-th component of the output with respect

968
00:45:39,250 --> 00:45:41,570
to the j-th component of the input.

969
00:45:41,570 --> 00:45:46,930
It turns out that, that is Wij.

970
00:45:46,930 --> 00:45:51,050
And so that actually means that this is really

971
00:45:51,050 --> 00:45:52,930
just the weights again.

972
00:45:52,930 --> 00:45:57,430
So this is really nice and simple, right?

973
00:46:00,330 --> 00:46:03,010
In the opposite direction, you multiply the gradient

974
00:46:03,010 --> 00:46:08,570
by the weights to get the gradient coming in.

975
00:46:08,570 --> 00:46:12,050
Cool, so your forward and backward pass

976
00:46:12,050 --> 00:46:14,850
are just multiplying by your weight matrix,

977
00:46:14,850 --> 00:46:16,810
just in a different order.

978
00:46:16,810 --> 00:46:18,890
Pretty nice.

979
00:46:18,890 --> 00:46:23,290
And finally, now let's see how we

980
00:46:23,290 --> 00:46:27,350
use those outputs to compute the weights update equation.

981
00:46:27,350 --> 00:46:30,810
So how do we actually do that backprop through to the weights?

982
00:46:30,810 --> 00:46:35,590
So that looks like this, right?

983
00:46:35,590 --> 00:46:38,290
We're trying to take the partial derivative of the cost

984
00:46:38,290 --> 00:46:40,210
with respect to those weights.

985
00:46:40,210 --> 00:46:43,050
And so that's going to be the output gradient multiplied

986
00:46:43,050 --> 00:46:44,670
by this partial derivative.

987
00:46:47,690 --> 00:46:50,650
If we look at the parameter Wij and look

988
00:46:50,650 --> 00:46:53,970
at how that parameter changes the cost,

989
00:46:53,970 --> 00:46:57,490
only the Ith component of the output is going to change.

990
00:46:57,490 --> 00:47:00,810
So this means that, essentially, you

991
00:47:00,810 --> 00:47:08,330
can break this down through this simple almost decomposition.

992
00:47:08,330 --> 00:47:13,690
And you end up getting this because of what we just

993
00:47:13,690 --> 00:47:19,170
pointed out, so essentially the partial derivative of the output

994
00:47:19,170 --> 00:47:25,770
at that i-th component with respect to the weights matrix.

995
00:47:25,770 --> 00:47:29,130
The corresponding element ij is actually just going

996
00:47:29,130 --> 00:47:31,770
to be equal to the input of j.

997
00:47:31,770 --> 00:47:36,120
And so that means the following.

998
00:47:36,120 --> 00:47:38,520
Essentially, when you want to calculate the update

999
00:47:38,520 --> 00:47:40,560
to the weights, all you're doing is

1000
00:47:40,560 --> 00:47:44,960
you're multiplying the input to the function

1001
00:47:44,960 --> 00:47:47,040
by the output gradient.

1002
00:47:47,040 --> 00:47:50,520
Again, it's a really nice simplification

1003
00:47:50,520 --> 00:47:54,200
that comes out just because everything is linear.

1004
00:47:54,200 --> 00:47:56,880
So then when we actually want to update the weights,

1005
00:47:56,880 --> 00:48:02,680
you're literally just taking eta, and transposing this,

1006
00:48:02,680 --> 00:48:08,400
and multiplying it or adding it in to the weights.

1007
00:48:08,400 --> 00:48:12,640
Any questions about of very nice, simple reformulation

1008
00:48:12,640 --> 00:48:15,480
that gives us essentially a bunch of matrix multiplications?

1009
00:48:15,480 --> 00:48:16,980
Yes?

1010
00:48:19,885 --> 00:48:21,260
AUDIENCE: Do we have enough time?

1011
00:48:21,260 --> 00:48:23,040
Can you work through a small example

1012
00:48:23,040 --> 00:48:26,480
with the computational graph on the board or something?

1013
00:48:26,480 --> 00:48:28,200
SARA BEERY: So at the end of the lecture,

1014
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:30,700
I actually do have an example of that.

1015
00:48:30,700 --> 00:48:33,880
And so either we'll get to it at the end--

1016
00:48:33,880 --> 00:48:35,320
I'm a bit worried about time--

1017
00:48:35,320 --> 00:48:36,633
or it's in the slides.

1018
00:48:36,633 --> 00:48:38,300
And so you can actually work through it.

1019
00:48:38,300 --> 00:48:40,560
And there are the solutions at the end.

1020
00:48:40,560 --> 00:48:41,880
Sounds good?

1021
00:48:41,880 --> 00:48:42,840
Cool.

1022
00:48:42,840 --> 00:48:47,280
All right, so cheat sheet for a linear layer.

1023
00:48:47,280 --> 00:48:51,440
The output is equal to the weights times the input.

1024
00:48:51,440 --> 00:48:53,480
The gradient in is equal to the output

1025
00:48:53,480 --> 00:48:55,320
gradient times the weights.

1026
00:48:55,320 --> 00:48:58,280
And the update to the weights is equal to the input

1027
00:48:58,280 --> 00:49:01,120
times the output gradient.

1028
00:49:01,120 --> 00:49:05,653
And then you actually do that update based on adding in that,

1029
00:49:05,653 --> 00:49:07,320
basically, direction you want to move in

1030
00:49:07,320 --> 00:49:10,600
multiplied by your learning rate.

1031
00:49:10,600 --> 00:49:13,492
So now let's look at a whole multilayer perceptron.

1032
00:49:13,492 --> 00:49:15,700
What happens if we put all these operations together?

1033
00:49:15,700 --> 00:49:18,920
So first, you have some linear layer.

1034
00:49:18,920 --> 00:49:21,660
Then, you'll have some ReLU, some activation function.

1035
00:49:21,660 --> 00:49:24,080
In particular, here, we're talking about ReLU.

1036
00:49:24,080 --> 00:49:26,180
Then, you have another linear layer.

1037
00:49:26,180 --> 00:49:29,190
So now this will be weights one, weights two.

1038
00:49:29,190 --> 00:49:30,750
And then you calculate some loss,

1039
00:49:30,750 --> 00:49:34,070
based on the difference between your output and the ground

1040
00:49:34,070 --> 00:49:35,390
truth.

1041
00:49:35,390 --> 00:49:38,750
So now to do a backwards pass, you essentially

1042
00:49:38,750 --> 00:49:42,110
assume a slight change in convention.

1043
00:49:42,110 --> 00:49:44,550
This will clarify this nice connection

1044
00:49:44,550 --> 00:49:46,830
between forward and backward directions.

1045
00:49:46,830 --> 00:49:49,077
So instead of representing gradients as row vectors,

1046
00:49:49,077 --> 00:49:50,910
we're going to transpose them and treat them

1047
00:49:50,910 --> 00:49:52,870
as column vectors.

1048
00:49:52,870 --> 00:49:54,390
And then that backwards operation

1049
00:49:54,390 --> 00:49:57,230
for the transposed vectors will follow from that matrix

1050
00:49:57,230 --> 00:50:01,430
identity, that AB transpose is B transpose A transpose, if you

1051
00:50:01,430 --> 00:50:05,070
remember basic matrix math.

1052
00:50:05,070 --> 00:50:07,950
So essentially, this then reveals

1053
00:50:07,950 --> 00:50:11,390
this interesting connection between forward and backward.

1054
00:50:11,390 --> 00:50:14,550
So backward for a linear layer is the same operation

1055
00:50:14,550 --> 00:50:17,230
as forward but with the weights transposed.

1056
00:50:17,230 --> 00:50:23,030
So here, now you're going backward, you get,

1057
00:50:23,030 --> 00:50:26,990
again, weights transposed times the gradient.

1058
00:50:26,990 --> 00:50:29,510
And then this is an interesting thing.

1059
00:50:29,510 --> 00:50:31,750
A ReLU wouldn't be a ReLU on the backwards pass

1060
00:50:31,750 --> 00:50:36,710
because you don't want to re pass them through ReLU.

1061
00:50:36,710 --> 00:50:38,550
Essentially, what you need to turn it into

1062
00:50:38,550 --> 00:50:42,030
is roughly like a gating matrix.

1063
00:50:42,030 --> 00:50:44,550
This will be parameterized by the function

1064
00:50:44,550 --> 00:50:45,990
by the function of the activations

1065
00:50:45,990 --> 00:50:47,790
from that forward pass.

1066
00:50:47,790 --> 00:50:50,550
So this would be like a, b, and c, the activations

1067
00:50:50,550 --> 00:50:53,190
that you had sending those weights through the ReLU

1068
00:50:53,190 --> 00:50:54,470
on the forward pass.

1069
00:50:54,470 --> 00:50:56,430
What this is doing is making sure

1070
00:50:56,430 --> 00:50:58,430
that you're not passing gradients

1071
00:50:58,430 --> 00:51:00,550
for the components of the weights that

1072
00:51:00,550 --> 00:51:10,490
corresponded to the parts that were in that 0 part of the ReLU.

1073
00:51:10,490 --> 00:51:12,830
You don't want to send gradients for parts

1074
00:51:12,830 --> 00:51:14,470
that should be masked out.

1075
00:51:14,470 --> 00:51:15,170
Yeah?

1076
00:51:15,170 --> 00:51:18,190
AUDIENCE: Why is that matrix diagonalized?

1077
00:51:18,190 --> 00:51:22,310
SARA BEERY: That's just so that it's operating on each element

1078
00:51:22,310 --> 00:51:25,150
independently.

1079
00:51:25,150 --> 00:51:26,500
Cool.

1080
00:51:26,500 --> 00:51:31,840
And then, now, you can mask the values of the gradients,

1081
00:51:31,840 --> 00:51:34,180
make sure you're not sending gradients

1082
00:51:34,180 --> 00:51:36,320
for things that are going into that 0,

1083
00:51:36,320 --> 00:51:38,420
that negative part of the ReLU.

1084
00:51:38,420 --> 00:51:40,940
And then, essentially, what this means

1085
00:51:40,940 --> 00:51:46,940
is that backprop is still a linear model.

1086
00:51:46,940 --> 00:51:50,300
Because even that ReLU, this is still a linear operation

1087
00:51:50,300 --> 00:51:52,220
that we've taken.

1088
00:51:52,220 --> 00:51:55,180
And there's a cool intuition.

1089
00:51:55,180 --> 00:51:58,090
Phil told this to me, and I really liked it.

1090
00:51:58,090 --> 00:51:59,840
If you're thinking about a loss landscape,

1091
00:51:59,840 --> 00:52:02,060
no matter how complex that loss landscape is,

1092
00:52:02,060 --> 00:52:05,440
if we're taking a first order approximation of the derivative,

1093
00:52:05,440 --> 00:52:07,140
essentially, what we're doing is we're

1094
00:52:07,140 --> 00:52:11,498
fitting a plane to this complex curved loss landscape,

1095
00:52:11,498 --> 00:52:13,540
and then we're moving the direction of the plane.

1096
00:52:13,540 --> 00:52:15,580
So by definition, it must be linear.

1097
00:52:15,580 --> 00:52:17,780
We're moving on a planar surface,

1098
00:52:17,780 --> 00:52:21,420
even if the actual underlying loss is not planar.

1099
00:52:21,420 --> 00:52:23,580
Make sense?

1100
00:52:23,580 --> 00:52:24,960
Cool.

1101
00:52:24,960 --> 00:52:29,300
So this means that forward and backward for a linear model is

1102
00:52:29,300 --> 00:52:35,180
actually all just one really big, flipped out linear model.

1103
00:52:35,180 --> 00:52:41,180
You can treat the entire thing as a single iteration, as just

1104
00:52:41,180 --> 00:52:42,740
one big linear model.

1105
00:52:42,740 --> 00:52:45,660
And it can be thought of as a single forward pass, in a way,

1106
00:52:45,660 --> 00:52:48,100
even though the actual had a forward pass is only

1107
00:52:48,100 --> 00:52:51,140
the first half, and the backward passes the second half.

1108
00:52:51,140 --> 00:52:52,573
Yeah?

1109
00:52:52,573 --> 00:52:54,240
AUDIENCE: Comparing forward and backward

1110
00:52:54,240 --> 00:52:57,600
pass, you said that to compute the backward pass,

1111
00:52:57,600 --> 00:53:00,480
you need to have the x in at each layer.

1112
00:53:00,480 --> 00:53:05,680
So I think that you need to have in memory for all the layers

1113
00:53:05,680 --> 00:53:07,820
what is the value of the embedding.

1114
00:53:07,820 --> 00:53:10,260
But as in the forward pass, you don't need it.

1115
00:53:10,260 --> 00:53:13,620
You only need it for the-- you can

1116
00:53:13,620 --> 00:53:18,060
discard the previous embedding, for instance,

1117
00:53:18,060 --> 00:53:20,980
with your [INAUDIBLE].

1118
00:53:20,980 --> 00:53:22,957
Once it has passed through the first layer,

1119
00:53:22,957 --> 00:53:25,290
then you can just erase the [INAUDIBLE] from your memory

1120
00:53:25,290 --> 00:53:26,730
as you're only going forward.

1121
00:53:26,730 --> 00:53:31,530
Whereas in the backward pass, you need to save all of them.

1122
00:53:31,530 --> 00:53:32,330
SARA BEERY: Yes.

1123
00:53:32,330 --> 00:53:36,250
So you do need to save the intermediate representation so

1124
00:53:36,250 --> 00:53:38,990
that you can efficiently compute back propagation.

1125
00:53:38,990 --> 00:53:39,550
That's true.

1126
00:53:39,550 --> 00:53:42,175
AUDIENCE: So there is not real symmetry [INAUDIBLE] the forward

1127
00:53:42,175 --> 00:53:43,290
and backward passes.

1128
00:53:43,290 --> 00:53:44,130
SARA BEERY: No.

1129
00:53:44,130 --> 00:53:46,338
But but, I mean, you can actually even see that here.

1130
00:53:46,338 --> 00:53:51,330
There's this asymmetry in where the information is flowing.

1131
00:53:51,330 --> 00:53:52,730
Does that make sense?

1132
00:53:52,730 --> 00:53:54,410
AUDIENCE: Yes.

1133
00:53:54,410 --> 00:53:55,410
SARA BEERY: Yeah?

1134
00:53:55,410 --> 00:53:58,730
AUDIENCE: Is there a specific reason why we don't see

1135
00:53:58,730 --> 00:54:00,330
[INAUDIBLE]?

1136
00:54:00,330 --> 00:54:02,930
SARA BEERY: Oh, that was just for simplicity here.

1137
00:54:02,930 --> 00:54:04,930
So, yeah, in practice, there would

1138
00:54:04,930 --> 00:54:06,670
be bias terms floating around everywhere,

1139
00:54:06,670 --> 00:54:10,050
but we just wanted to capture the intuition.

1140
00:54:10,050 --> 00:54:11,770
Yeah?

1141
00:54:11,770 --> 00:54:22,580
AUDIENCE: When we're going back through our ReLU layer,

1142
00:54:22,580 --> 00:54:26,650
do we need to know the pre-activations--

1143
00:54:26,650 --> 00:54:28,180
we need to have that in memory?

1144
00:54:28,180 --> 00:54:29,930
SARA BEERY: You basically need to save off

1145
00:54:29,930 --> 00:54:32,010
what the value of the activations

1146
00:54:32,010 --> 00:54:34,140
were for each component of your input.

1147
00:54:34,140 --> 00:54:35,890
And you keep those in memory, and then you

1148
00:54:35,890 --> 00:54:39,890
build that sparse matrix that lets you actually

1149
00:54:39,890 --> 00:54:42,510
gate those going back through.

1150
00:54:42,510 --> 00:54:43,010
Yeah.

1151
00:54:43,010 --> 00:54:43,870
Cool.

1152
00:54:43,870 --> 00:54:44,370
OK.

1153
00:54:44,370 --> 00:54:49,170
So let's move on to talking about DAGs.

1154
00:54:49,170 --> 00:54:52,250
So what if your DAG isn't a chain?

1155
00:54:52,250 --> 00:54:54,250
And actually, this is pretty common.

1156
00:54:54,250 --> 00:55:00,170
We often have connections within our machine-learning models

1157
00:55:00,170 --> 00:55:04,030
that are not just chains, for example,

1158
00:55:04,030 --> 00:55:06,130
sharing weights, or splitting weights,

1159
00:55:06,130 --> 00:55:09,970
or having things come together, be concatenated

1160
00:55:09,970 --> 00:55:16,250
from different heads or being split into different objectives.

1161
00:55:16,250 --> 00:55:19,320
So let's talk about what this actually looks like.

1162
00:55:19,320 --> 00:55:21,520
So there's actually only two operations

1163
00:55:21,520 --> 00:55:23,840
that you need to make everything we just

1164
00:55:23,840 --> 00:55:26,960
talked about work for any arbitrary directed-acyclic

1165
00:55:26,960 --> 00:55:27,500
graph.

1166
00:55:27,500 --> 00:55:29,520
And those are merging and branching.

1167
00:55:29,520 --> 00:55:32,697
So assuming you have some merge where, here--

1168
00:55:32,697 --> 00:55:34,780
and that merge could be a lot of different things.

1169
00:55:34,780 --> 00:55:35,655
It could be addition.

1170
00:55:35,655 --> 00:55:38,080
It could be concatenation, what have you.

1171
00:55:38,080 --> 00:55:42,320
You have some components, some functional components

1172
00:55:42,320 --> 00:55:45,000
that you're merging together to move forward

1173
00:55:45,000 --> 00:55:48,350
through your directed-acyclic graph.

1174
00:55:48,350 --> 00:55:50,600
Now, if you actually need to propagate a gradient back

1175
00:55:50,600 --> 00:55:53,280
through that, essentially, you just

1176
00:55:53,280 --> 00:55:55,760
need to make sure that you track the gradient with respect

1177
00:55:55,760 --> 00:55:57,340
to the correct input variables.

1178
00:55:57,340 --> 00:56:01,960
So if, here, you just only pass the gradient that

1179
00:56:01,960 --> 00:56:07,280
corresponds to the x superscript a, and on the other path,

1180
00:56:07,280 --> 00:56:10,600
you only pass the gradient that corresponds to the components

1181
00:56:10,600 --> 00:56:13,400
of that other part of the path.

1182
00:56:13,400 --> 00:56:17,440
Similarly, if you're branching, so now you're

1183
00:56:17,440 --> 00:56:22,000
splitting up your information in some way or even just

1184
00:56:22,000 --> 00:56:23,100
duplicating it.

1185
00:56:23,100 --> 00:56:25,580
Now, if you're propagating a gradient back through that,

1186
00:56:25,580 --> 00:56:27,280
it's as simple as a sum.

1187
00:56:27,280 --> 00:56:30,240
You just take the gradients from both, and you sum them,

1188
00:56:30,240 --> 00:56:33,480
and you send that back through that input.

1189
00:56:33,480 --> 00:56:34,560
Cool.

1190
00:56:34,560 --> 00:56:38,440
So there's a much more detailed derivation in the lecture notes,

1191
00:56:38,440 --> 00:56:40,520
if you're interested.

1192
00:56:40,520 --> 00:56:42,080
But so now let's talk about what this

1193
00:56:42,080 --> 00:56:43,800
means for parameter sharing.

1194
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:46,140
So say you have some chain.

1195
00:56:46,140 --> 00:56:48,640
And now you have the parameters are

1196
00:56:48,640 --> 00:56:52,400
separate for each component of that chain.

1197
00:56:52,400 --> 00:56:55,160
You might instead have something where

1198
00:56:55,160 --> 00:56:56,680
those parameters are actually shared

1199
00:56:56,680 --> 00:56:58,480
across those different components.

1200
00:56:58,480 --> 00:57:00,200
Maybe you're reusing parameters that

1201
00:57:00,200 --> 00:57:02,480
were pre-trained on ImageNet or something,

1202
00:57:02,480 --> 00:57:05,920
and you're using them in multiple parts of your network.

1203
00:57:05,920 --> 00:57:07,760
There, again, you can actually just

1204
00:57:07,760 --> 00:57:10,120
think about flipping this on its side.

1205
00:57:10,120 --> 00:57:11,767
All that is a branch, right?

1206
00:57:11,767 --> 00:57:13,600
So now, if you're passing your gradient back

1207
00:57:13,600 --> 00:57:15,870
through that branch, you just need

1208
00:57:15,870 --> 00:57:18,390
to be summing the gradients that are coming in

1209
00:57:18,390 --> 00:57:21,830
from these different branch dimensions.

1210
00:57:21,830 --> 00:57:24,790
So parameter sharing, some ingredients.

1211
00:57:24,790 --> 00:57:25,290
All right.

1212
00:57:25,290 --> 00:57:27,430
So towards the end of the lecture, now

1213
00:57:27,430 --> 00:57:30,790
I want to move on and talk about this kind of meta concept

1214
00:57:30,790 --> 00:57:32,590
of what differential programming is

1215
00:57:32,590 --> 00:57:35,590
and maybe why neural network training is, essentially,

1216
00:57:35,590 --> 00:57:38,070
just differential programming.

1217
00:57:38,070 --> 00:57:40,310
So when we think about deep learning,

1218
00:57:40,310 --> 00:57:42,150
generally, we think about it this way.

1219
00:57:42,150 --> 00:57:46,230
You have some network, again, a DAG.

1220
00:57:46,230 --> 00:57:48,950
And then you define these forward and backward passes

1221
00:57:48,950 --> 00:57:50,710
through that network.

1222
00:57:50,710 --> 00:57:52,930
Differential programming takes the same perspective,

1223
00:57:52,930 --> 00:57:58,350
but now we basically just are actually programming that model.

1224
00:57:58,350 --> 00:58:02,670
And this is really what all of these different existing

1225
00:58:02,670 --> 00:58:03,230
languages--

1226
00:58:03,230 --> 00:58:08,470
PyTorch, TensorFlow, if anyone still uses it, JAX--

1227
00:58:08,470 --> 00:58:12,110
these are all basically libraries that

1228
00:58:12,110 --> 00:58:16,550
enable us to really efficiently do differential programming.

1229
00:58:16,550 --> 00:58:19,510
So deep networks are popular for a few different reasons.

1230
00:58:19,510 --> 00:58:21,350
First, they're easy to optimize.

1231
00:58:21,350 --> 00:58:22,910
Everything is differentiable.

1232
00:58:22,910 --> 00:58:25,190
Second, they're compositional.

1233
00:58:25,190 --> 00:58:28,390
It's essentially like block-based programming.

1234
00:58:28,390 --> 00:58:31,390
And so differential programming surfaced

1235
00:58:31,390 --> 00:58:35,443
as an emerging term for general models with those properties.

1236
00:58:35,443 --> 00:58:38,110
These tweets are a bit old now, but I still think they're funny.

1237
00:58:38,110 --> 00:58:41,590
Yann LeCun said, "deep learning has outlived its usefulness

1238
00:58:41,590 --> 00:58:44,430
as a buzz phrase, deep learning, est mort.

1239
00:58:44,430 --> 00:58:46,470
Vive differential programming."

1240
00:58:46,470 --> 00:58:49,270
Essentially, even years ago, this

1241
00:58:49,270 --> 00:58:51,510
was like maybe this is the new buzzword for what

1242
00:58:51,510 --> 00:58:53,230
we're talking about here.

1243
00:58:53,230 --> 00:58:56,190
Tom Ditterich, who is significantly less splashy

1244
00:58:56,190 --> 00:58:59,230
in his tweets, said that, "deep learning is essentially

1245
00:58:59,230 --> 00:59:02,330
a new style of programming, differential programming.

1246
00:59:02,330 --> 00:59:03,750
And the field is trying to work up

1247
00:59:03,750 --> 00:59:07,022
what the reusable constructs in this style are.

1248
00:59:07,022 --> 00:59:08,730
And there are some that we actually know.

1249
00:59:08,730 --> 00:59:11,140
Some of these blocks have been well defined,

1250
00:59:11,140 --> 00:59:14,780
things like convolution, pooling, LSTMs, GANs, VAEs,

1251
00:59:14,780 --> 00:59:18,860
memory units, routing units, et cetera."

1252
00:59:18,860 --> 00:59:23,640
So this is taking that perspective.

1253
00:59:23,640 --> 00:59:26,872
This is a paper from 2017 called Neural Module Networks, where

1254
00:59:26,872 --> 00:59:28,580
essentially, here, you can see that there

1255
00:59:28,580 --> 00:59:31,360
are these different components of the model.

1256
00:59:31,360 --> 00:59:35,260
And some of them might be architectural, and some of them

1257
00:59:35,260 --> 00:59:38,740
might have parameters that are learned or not.

1258
00:59:38,740 --> 00:59:42,420
So you're sending in something like, where is the dog?

1259
00:59:42,420 --> 00:59:44,367
And then maybe that gets routed to a parser,

1260
00:59:44,367 --> 00:59:46,200
and that parser might not be learned at all.

1261
00:59:46,200 --> 00:59:48,480
It might just be a standard parser.

1262
00:59:48,480 --> 00:59:52,060
So that component might not have any differential parameters that

1263
00:59:52,060 --> 00:59:54,220
are actually being learned.

1264
00:59:54,220 --> 00:59:56,300
But then, potentially, there's a CNN

1265
00:59:56,300 --> 00:59:58,340
down here that's parsing the objects that

1266
00:59:58,340 --> 00:59:59,740
are seen in an image.

1267
00:59:59,740 --> 01:00:02,300
And that might actually be something

1268
01:00:02,300 --> 01:00:04,300
that you have updates being propagated to

1269
01:00:04,300 --> 01:00:06,540
from your training data set.

1270
01:00:06,540 --> 01:00:09,640
And there's an interesting blog post from Andrej Karpathy

1271
01:00:09,640 --> 01:00:13,580
that talks about this as what he calls Software 2.0.

1272
01:00:13,580 --> 01:00:16,100
Essentially, here, he's saying that Software 1.0, which

1273
01:00:16,100 --> 01:00:18,940
is the software we had originally, where everything

1274
01:00:18,940 --> 01:00:22,540
is defined, that might be just that little, red dot.

1275
01:00:22,540 --> 01:00:24,700
Software 2.0 is actually defining

1276
01:00:24,700 --> 01:00:30,000
a space of possible software systems,

1277
01:00:30,000 --> 01:00:32,380
and then you're actually optimizing within that space

1278
01:00:32,380 --> 01:00:38,260
to build the system that you need to solve your problem.

1279
01:00:38,260 --> 01:00:42,380
So here, instead of thinking about this DAG, where

1280
01:00:42,380 --> 01:00:46,340
every component is actually differentiable

1281
01:00:46,340 --> 01:00:48,360
or every component is differentiable--

1282
01:00:48,360 --> 01:00:50,120
where every component is learned,

1283
01:00:50,120 --> 01:00:52,900
instead, you might have a bunch of the components of your system

1284
01:00:52,900 --> 01:00:55,300
be explicitly programmed by a human.

1285
01:00:55,300 --> 01:00:59,660
A really obvious example of this is we often explicitly program

1286
01:00:59,660 --> 01:01:03,100
the normalization values that we want to use for natural images

1287
01:01:03,100 --> 01:01:05,620
directly into the pre-processing of our data.

1288
01:01:05,620 --> 01:01:07,490
This is something that we just define.

1289
01:01:07,490 --> 01:01:10,250
We don't learn what those normalization values should be.

1290
01:01:10,250 --> 01:01:13,090
And then there are these other components

1291
01:01:13,090 --> 01:01:18,970
where this part is actually programmed by back propagation,

1292
01:01:18,970 --> 01:01:21,330
so, for example, programmed by tuning the behavior

1293
01:01:21,330 --> 01:01:23,770
to match the training examples.

1294
01:01:23,770 --> 01:01:25,850
And the cool thing is because we assume

1295
01:01:25,850 --> 01:01:29,050
that every piece of this system is differentiable,

1296
01:01:29,050 --> 01:01:32,690
you can actually optimize any node or any edge

1297
01:01:32,690 --> 01:01:35,650
with regard to any scalar cost.

1298
01:01:35,650 --> 01:01:40,810
So here, you could think about calculating how the cost would

1299
01:01:40,810 --> 01:01:46,010
change when the weights of the specific yellow function change.

1300
01:01:46,010 --> 01:01:49,570
You could also think about looking

1301
01:01:49,570 --> 01:01:52,910
at how the cost changes when the input data changes, right?

1302
01:01:52,910 --> 01:01:54,730
You don't actually need to specifically do

1303
01:01:54,730 --> 01:01:57,190
this for weights.

1304
01:01:57,190 --> 01:01:58,010
Yeah?

1305
01:01:58,010 --> 01:02:02,890
AUDIENCE: So is this how we create a metric that

1306
01:02:02,890 --> 01:02:06,570
can determine what might be good for a human to train versus

1307
01:02:06,570 --> 01:02:07,670
the computer to train?

1308
01:02:07,670 --> 01:02:10,050
Or is there another qualitative or empirical metric

1309
01:02:10,050 --> 01:02:11,770
that we might use?

1310
01:02:11,770 --> 01:02:14,170
SARA BEERY: So a big question.

1311
01:02:14,170 --> 01:02:17,330
So he was asking if this is something

1312
01:02:17,330 --> 01:02:21,010
that helps us build metrics around what maybe we might want

1313
01:02:21,010 --> 01:02:26,130
to optimize via data versus what we want to just define directly.

1314
01:02:26,130 --> 01:02:29,970
I think, experimentally, we often find those things out

1315
01:02:29,970 --> 01:02:31,930
for different applications.

1316
01:02:31,930 --> 01:02:35,250
What parts does it actually good to--

1317
01:02:35,250 --> 01:02:39,190
essentially, anytime a human is programming part of this system,

1318
01:02:39,190 --> 01:02:40,790
it's constraining the system.

1319
01:02:40,790 --> 01:02:42,430
And that constraint can be useful,

1320
01:02:42,430 --> 01:02:43,830
but it could also be unuseful.

1321
01:02:43,830 --> 01:02:46,930
I think about when I started in machine learning,

1322
01:02:46,930 --> 01:02:47,890
it was built around--

1323
01:02:47,890 --> 01:02:49,390
I'm going to date myself right now--

1324
01:02:49,390 --> 01:02:51,950
but we did a lot of what we called feature engineering,

1325
01:02:51,950 --> 01:02:53,690
which is essentially like us building

1326
01:02:53,690 --> 01:02:56,650
a bunch of explicit constraints into how the data might be

1327
01:02:56,650 --> 01:03:00,690
processed to then go through a very, very simple neural network

1328
01:03:00,690 --> 01:03:04,120
or even something more simple than that, something

1329
01:03:04,120 --> 01:03:06,240
like an SDM.

1330
01:03:06,240 --> 01:03:08,720
And those bottlenecks, it turned out,

1331
01:03:08,720 --> 01:03:10,420
were not necessarily optimal.

1332
01:03:10,420 --> 01:03:11,932
We were defining this.

1333
01:03:11,932 --> 01:03:13,640
We were programming it, but it turned out

1334
01:03:13,640 --> 01:03:18,580
our best ideas were not as good as just a much more complex,

1335
01:03:18,580 --> 01:03:21,185
larger models that were learned somewhat end-to-end.

1336
01:03:21,185 --> 01:03:22,060
Does that make sense?

1337
01:03:22,060 --> 01:03:23,540
So I think the jury's still out.

1338
01:03:23,540 --> 01:03:25,290
When you talk about metrics, that's really

1339
01:03:25,290 --> 01:03:27,400
where that red square comes in.

1340
01:03:27,400 --> 01:03:29,680
You define what that cost function is.

1341
01:03:29,680 --> 01:03:34,240
So you have to figure out how you are going to define the loss

1342
01:03:34,240 --> 01:03:36,260
and what that then looks like for your cost.

1343
01:03:36,260 --> 01:03:40,860
And that definition will affect what your optimal are.

1344
01:03:40,860 --> 01:03:41,360
Cool.

1345
01:03:41,360 --> 01:03:45,360
So let's talk a little bit about optimizing parameters

1346
01:03:45,360 --> 01:03:47,960
versus optimizing inputs.

1347
01:03:47,960 --> 01:03:50,765
So this is the standard approach, right?

1348
01:03:50,765 --> 01:03:52,640
This is looking at how much the total cost is

1349
01:03:52,640 --> 01:03:55,380
increased or decreased by changing the parameters.

1350
01:03:55,380 --> 01:03:58,280
This is all the different parameters

1351
01:03:58,280 --> 01:04:00,000
throughout the network.

1352
01:04:00,000 --> 01:04:02,600
This is what we commonly think of,

1353
01:04:02,600 --> 01:04:08,520
but you can also think about optimizing the input for a fixed

1354
01:04:08,520 --> 01:04:11,440
set of parameters.

1355
01:04:11,440 --> 01:04:15,200
So here, this could be something how much the chameleon

1356
01:04:15,200 --> 01:04:20,360
score is increased or decreased by changing the input pixels.

1357
01:04:20,360 --> 01:04:26,040
And so here, now, if you think about this from a way

1358
01:04:26,040 --> 01:04:28,960
where you're optimizing maybe class logits before the softmax

1359
01:04:28,960 --> 01:04:32,400
or optimizing the class probabilities after the softmax,

1360
01:04:32,400 --> 01:04:36,480
the maybe easiest way to increase the probability softmax

1361
01:04:36,480 --> 01:04:40,160
given to a class is often to make the alternatives unlikely,

1362
01:04:40,160 --> 01:04:43,040
rather than to make the class of interest likely.

1363
01:04:43,040 --> 01:04:45,460
Whereas if you optimize pre softmax logits,

1364
01:04:45,460 --> 01:04:47,720
this tends to actually be a bit more stable.

1365
01:04:47,720 --> 01:04:49,680
So what we find is if you want to actually make

1366
01:04:49,680 --> 01:04:52,960
an image that maximizes the cat output neuron--

1367
01:04:52,960 --> 01:04:57,040
again, we are doing this maybe pre softmax--

1368
01:04:57,040 --> 01:04:58,580
it might look something like this.

1369
01:04:58,580 --> 01:05:01,350
So this is an interesting, again,

1370
01:05:01,350 --> 01:05:04,870
blog post back from 2017, but on feature visualization.

1371
01:05:04,870 --> 01:05:08,230
So this is telling you what a given trained model thinks

1372
01:05:08,230 --> 01:05:11,910
is most cat like.

1373
01:05:11,910 --> 01:05:13,950
And this is something you can learn.

1374
01:05:13,950 --> 01:05:17,390
Or you can actually also do things

1375
01:05:17,390 --> 01:05:19,790
that say, for example, make an image that maximizes

1376
01:05:19,790 --> 01:05:23,710
the value of a neuron, j, on a layer, L.

1377
01:05:23,710 --> 01:05:27,990
So using this as a mechanism to probe what the model is paying

1378
01:05:27,990 --> 01:05:29,110
attention to.

1379
01:05:29,110 --> 01:05:31,950
So that might look something like this.

1380
01:05:31,950 --> 01:05:34,150
This is saying that this particular neuron

1381
01:05:34,150 --> 01:05:37,870
and this particular layer is looking for stuff that looks

1382
01:05:37,870 --> 01:05:40,630
like this in a hand-wavy way.

1383
01:05:40,630 --> 01:05:43,985
And this is the idea behind, for example, DeepDream.

1384
01:05:43,985 --> 01:05:46,610
I don't know if any of you saw this, a couple of years old now.

1385
01:05:46,610 --> 01:05:49,870
But I think these are psychedelic and beautiful.

1386
01:05:49,870 --> 01:05:56,790
And this is looking at what these models, I guess, dream of.

1387
01:05:56,790 --> 01:06:00,605
And this is an idea of what's to come.

1388
01:06:00,605 --> 01:06:02,230
But if you're comfortable with the idea

1389
01:06:02,230 --> 01:06:05,670
that you can optimize anything with respect to anything,

1390
01:06:05,670 --> 01:06:09,070
here, we're looking at maximizing clip

1391
01:06:09,070 --> 01:06:12,910
as a model that tries to make descriptions

1392
01:06:12,910 --> 01:06:15,270
and images that correspond to them

1393
01:06:15,270 --> 01:06:17,990
close together in a joint embedding space.

1394
01:06:17,990 --> 01:06:20,710
So you want to build a text encoder and an image encoder

1395
01:06:20,710 --> 01:06:23,370
where things that are similar, semantically,

1396
01:06:23,370 --> 01:06:25,350
from text to images, are quite close

1397
01:06:25,350 --> 01:06:28,270
together in the learned embedding space, that projection

1398
01:06:28,270 --> 01:06:30,230
that you're projecting each of them into.

1399
01:06:30,230 --> 01:06:32,670
Now, if you have something like CLIP,

1400
01:06:32,670 --> 01:06:39,670
then you can, for example, tie your CLIP model together,

1401
01:06:39,670 --> 01:06:42,270
the image encoder and the text encoder from your CLIP model,

1402
01:06:42,270 --> 01:06:45,510
together with a GAN, something like a generative adversarial

1403
01:06:45,510 --> 01:06:46,470
network.

1404
01:06:46,470 --> 01:06:48,910
And then you can optimize the embedding

1405
01:06:48,910 --> 01:06:52,510
or that input parameter that goes into that image generator.

1406
01:06:52,510 --> 01:06:56,580
And you can figure out, for a given input,

1407
01:06:56,580 --> 01:06:59,780
how to optimize this so that your output of your image

1408
01:06:59,780 --> 01:07:03,620
generator is the thing that's closest in CLIP space

1409
01:07:03,620 --> 01:07:07,260
to any given prompt.

1410
01:07:07,260 --> 01:07:10,620
And if you actually look at what that learns over time,

1411
01:07:10,620 --> 01:07:13,500
you can get stuff that's fascinating.

1412
01:07:13,500 --> 01:07:17,500
So this is a way to actually generate an image from text.

1413
01:07:17,500 --> 01:07:20,360
And here, again, all these trapezoids are neural networks.

1414
01:07:20,360 --> 01:07:21,520
You can plug them together.

1415
01:07:21,520 --> 01:07:23,500
You can take the components trained in one way

1416
01:07:23,500 --> 01:07:25,340
and use them in another way.

1417
01:07:25,340 --> 01:07:27,740
Really, the idea is you can optimize modules

1418
01:07:27,740 --> 01:07:30,020
with respect to all the other modules,

1419
01:07:30,020 --> 01:07:33,500
and the world's your oyster.

1420
01:07:33,500 --> 01:07:34,980
Cool.

1421
01:07:34,980 --> 01:07:36,900
So what we covered today--

1422
01:07:36,900 --> 01:07:39,220
review of gradient descent and SGD,

1423
01:07:39,220 --> 01:07:41,880
computation graphs, backprop through chains,

1424
01:07:41,880 --> 01:07:45,820
backprop through MLPs, backprop through DAGs,

1425
01:07:45,820 --> 01:07:47,980
and differential programming.

1426
01:07:47,980 --> 01:07:49,080
Any questions?

1427
01:07:54,420 --> 01:07:55,443
