1428
01:07:55,443 --> 01:07:57,360
AUDIENCE: I have a question about [INAUDIBLE].

1429
01:08:02,540 --> 01:08:05,620
So first, why do you have to train the network [INAUDIBLE]?

1430
01:08:12,260 --> 01:08:14,260
SARA BEERY: So essentially what we're doing here

1431
01:08:14,260 --> 01:08:15,720
is you had CLIP.

1432
01:08:15,720 --> 01:08:17,899
You have this text encoder and this image encoder.

1433
01:08:17,899 --> 01:08:20,995
And they were already trained, and now they're fixed, right?

1434
01:08:20,995 --> 01:08:23,620
So the idea is, here, the text and the image encoder, actually,

1435
01:08:23,620 --> 01:08:25,700
we're not backpropping through them at all.

1436
01:08:25,700 --> 01:08:27,220
They're now defined.

1437
01:08:27,220 --> 01:08:28,740
They're not being learned.

1438
01:08:28,740 --> 01:08:31,660
Similarly, you've separately trained an image generation

1439
01:08:31,660 --> 01:08:33,899
model that takes in any given embedding

1440
01:08:33,899 --> 01:08:36,819
and generates an image from that embedding.

1441
01:08:36,819 --> 01:08:39,260
Now that one is also fixed.

1442
01:08:39,260 --> 01:08:41,517
So now you have a lot of parameters in a model,

1443
01:08:41,517 --> 01:08:42,600
but all of them are fixed.

1444
01:08:42,600 --> 01:08:44,180
And the only thing you're optimizing

1445
01:08:44,180 --> 01:08:50,540
is this hidden parameter that's going to be the input

1446
01:08:50,540 --> 01:08:55,490
to the image generator, such that this is as is maximal.

1447
01:08:55,490 --> 01:08:59,250
So you're trying to find the sine of z value

1448
01:08:59,250 --> 01:09:02,830
that maximizes this with respect to that input text.

1449
01:09:02,830 --> 01:09:08,580
AUDIENCE: So

1450
01:09:08,580 --> 01:09:09,330
SARA BEERY: Sorry?

1451
01:09:09,330 --> 01:09:15,050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] optimization [INAUDIBLE]?

1452
01:09:15,050 --> 01:09:17,370
SARA BEERY: Yeah, so all the parameters here are fixed.

1453
01:09:17,370 --> 01:09:20,689
But you are going to need to calculate

1454
01:09:20,689 --> 01:09:23,370
how to backprop the signal back through these models

1455
01:09:23,370 --> 01:09:26,250
to get to that one.

1456
01:09:26,250 --> 01:09:27,609
Yeah?

1457
01:09:27,609 --> 01:09:30,820
AUDIENCE: Can you explain more about embedding [INAUDIBLE]?

1458
01:09:30,820 --> 01:09:32,090
SARA BEERY: Yeah, absolutely.

1459
01:09:32,090 --> 01:09:33,970
So when I say embedding--

1460
01:09:33,970 --> 01:09:37,529
I might also say representation--

1461
01:09:37,529 --> 01:09:40,729
these are all terminology that we often

1462
01:09:40,729 --> 01:09:43,770
will throw around in the machine-learning community.

1463
01:09:43,770 --> 01:09:47,170
But usually it means something like this.

1464
01:09:47,170 --> 01:09:51,290
So you have some neural network.

1465
01:09:51,290 --> 01:09:53,529
And we'll call them an encoder sometimes,

1466
01:09:53,529 --> 01:09:55,250
again, just terminology.

1467
01:09:55,250 --> 01:09:59,050
But this neural network is essentially building a mapping

1468
01:09:59,050 --> 01:10:02,150
from a high-dimensional input or a complex input,

1469
01:10:02,150 --> 01:10:06,050
something like an image, to a low-dimensional representation

1470
01:10:06,050 --> 01:10:10,210
or a low-dimensional embedding of that input data.

1471
01:10:10,210 --> 01:10:15,270
And so that might be a vector of length 2048, for example,

1472
01:10:15,270 --> 01:10:16,190
or 1024.

1473
01:10:16,190 --> 01:10:20,650
Those are both common embedding sizes.

1474
01:10:20,650 --> 01:10:22,430
But really, when we talk about embedding,

1475
01:10:22,430 --> 01:10:24,410
it's essentially just saying we've trained the neural network

1476
01:10:24,410 --> 01:10:25,230
already.

1477
01:10:25,230 --> 01:10:29,450
Now we're using it to give us a low rank

1478
01:10:29,450 --> 01:10:31,650
representation of our data.

1479
01:10:31,650 --> 01:10:34,170
Does that make sense?

1480
01:10:34,170 --> 01:10:34,860
Yes?

1481
01:10:34,860 --> 01:10:36,610
AUDIENCE: I notice that some of the slides

1482
01:10:36,610 --> 01:10:39,170
have used loss and the others cost.

1483
01:10:39,170 --> 01:10:40,585
Are they interchangeable?

1484
01:10:40,585 --> 01:10:41,210
SARA BEERY: No.

1485
01:10:41,210 --> 01:10:43,530
This is me being a little fast and loose.

1486
01:10:43,530 --> 01:10:45,770
The way we defined it is that the loss is

1487
01:10:45,770 --> 01:10:48,630
like a function over all of the data points.

1488
01:10:48,630 --> 01:10:53,600
And then the cost might be the overall calculation

1489
01:10:53,600 --> 01:10:56,020
of that loss over, maybe, all of your data or something.

1490
01:10:56,020 --> 01:10:59,880
But I think it's a little semantic.

1491
01:10:59,880 --> 01:11:01,937
Yeah.

1492
01:11:01,937 --> 01:11:03,520
It's not the end of the world to think

1493
01:11:03,520 --> 01:11:05,100
of them somewhat interchangeably.

1494
01:11:05,100 --> 01:11:08,600
Essentially, both are you're defining some mechanism

1495
01:11:08,600 --> 01:11:12,880
for evaluating optimality.

1496
01:11:12,880 --> 01:11:13,703
Yeah?

1497
01:11:13,703 --> 01:11:15,120
AUDIENCE: How easy is it to switch

1498
01:11:15,120 --> 01:11:18,560
between optimizing parameters versus optimizing inputs?

1499
01:11:18,560 --> 01:11:22,500
SARA BEERY: So lucky for us, things like PyTorch exist.

1500
01:11:22,500 --> 01:11:24,520
And so actually this is just a matter

1501
01:11:24,520 --> 01:11:28,360
of defining what you want to update, basically

1502
01:11:28,360 --> 01:11:30,317
where you want to freeze your gradients,

1503
01:11:30,317 --> 01:11:32,400
and where you don't want to freeze your gradients.

1504
01:11:32,400 --> 01:11:34,220
And that's not actually so hard to do.

1505
01:11:34,220 --> 01:11:34,762
AUDIENCE: OK.

1506
01:11:34,762 --> 01:11:37,760
If we were to do that switch, could we use the prior work,

1507
01:11:37,760 --> 01:11:42,102
convert it in a way that we can use the prior [INAUDIBLE]?

1508
01:11:42,102 --> 01:11:43,060
SARA BEERY: Yeah, yeah.

1509
01:11:43,060 --> 01:11:45,700
So this is where that modularity comes in.

1510
01:11:45,700 --> 01:11:46,700
Sorry, guys.

1511
01:11:46,700 --> 01:11:48,740
If just if you could be quiet.

1512
01:11:48,740 --> 01:11:50,740
So this is where the modularity comes in, right?

1513
01:11:50,740 --> 01:11:59,040
So we talked about here, you can have these models where

1514
01:11:59,040 --> 01:12:01,000
some parts are programmed by a human

1515
01:12:01,000 --> 01:12:03,700
and some parts are actually being programmed by backprop.

1516
01:12:03,700 --> 01:12:05,360
But you could also think of the ones that are quote,

1517
01:12:05,360 --> 01:12:06,610
unquote programmed by a human.

1518
01:12:06,610 --> 01:12:08,320
Those might have just actually previously

1519
01:12:08,320 --> 01:12:09,540
been programmed by backprop.

1520
01:12:09,540 --> 01:12:12,480
And now you're taking those weights and plugging them in.

1521
